process.env.SKILL_ADVISOR_DEBUG` is set and otherwise a copy without `skillLabel` and `errorDetails`, and pass `{ requireDebug: false }` to `writeBoundedJsonl` in both cases. Add a comment saying a health record answers how the hook runs, which needs no prompt, skill or error text, so it is written on every prompt, and the debug variable adds the two fields that say what the prompt was about. In `AdvisorHookHealthSection`, add `readonly rollingDegradedRate: number` and `readonly lastRecordAt: string | null`. In `buildAdvisorHookHealthSection`, set `rollingDegradedRate` to the share of `lastInvocations` whose `degraded` is true (0 when there are none) and `lastRecordAt` to the `timestamp` of the last element of `lastInvocations`, or `null` when it is empty. In `STT`, add two cases after `reports hook health from durable diagnostic records`, each deleting `SKILL_ADVISOR_DEBUG` and restoring it afterwards. `writes a health record without the debug variable and keeps the skill label out` persists one record built with `skillLabel: 'sk-doc'`, `errorDetails: 'probe detail'` and `degraded: true`, reads the one line at `advisorHookDiagnosticsPath(root)` and expects no `skillLabel`, no `errorDetails`, `degraded` true and `readAdvisorStatus({ workspaceRoot: root }).hookHealth?.invocations` 1. `trims the default health log to 200 records` persists 301 records and expects 200 lines in that file. Verify: `V tests/handlers/advisor-status.vitest.ts tests/legacy/advisor-... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n94: - [ ] T013 [P] Give the hook metrics a degraded flag (`BRF`). In `AdvisorHookMetrics`, add `readonly degraded?: boolean` after `tokenCap`, with a one-line comment saying it is true when the answer came from the CLI's local scorer rather than the daemon, which freshness alone cannot tell. Verify: `TC` exits 0 and `rg -c \"readonly degraded\\?: boolean\" $BRF` prints 1.\n95: - [ ] T014 [P] Show the mode label only for a hub the prompt names (`CA`, `CAT`, `REC`, `RCT`). Give `routeNeedsMode` a third parameter `namedSkills: readonly string[]` and return `false` unless `namedSkills.includes(skillId)`, before the existing checks. Extend its comment: a hub that tops a prompt which never named it is the scorer's guess, so the label would point at modes the reader did not ask about. In `REC`, import `extractMetalinguisticSkillMentions` from `../lib/prompt-policy.js`, compute it once from `prompt` in `enrichCompiledRoutes` after the early return and pass it as the third argument of the one `routeNeedsMode` call. In `CAT`, inside `describe('a route that waits for a mode')`, pass the hub's own name as the third argument of each of the six calls, in order `['system-deep-loop']`, `['sk-doc']`, `['sk-doc']`, `['sk-code']`, `['sk-doc']` and `['sk-doc']`, so each keeps its result for its own reason, and add `expect(routeNeedsMode('sk-doc', { action: 'clarify' }, [])).toBe(false)` to the second case. In `RCT`, in `marks a hub that needs a mode without marking a hub that has none`, change the first prompt to `Use the sk-doc skill to write a README for the ledger module`, and after its three expectations run the same mock with `Write a README for the ledger module` and expect the `sk-doc` row to rank with no `needsMode` property. Verify: `V tests/command-attach.vitest.ts tests/handlers/advisor-recommend-compiled-route-option.vitest.ts` reports 0 failed, `TC` exits 0 and `rg -c \"routeNeedsMode\\(recommendation.skillId, compiledRoute, \" $REC` prints 1. When `sk-doc` does not rank for either prompt, mark the task `[B]` with the printed ranking.\n96: - [ ] T015 [P] Count an unsafe run whose line lists no changed path (`S`, `T`). Add `unsafeNoChangedPath: 0` to `newTally` and `unsafeNoChangedPath: tally.unsafeNoChangedPath` to `tallyBlock`, each right after `unsafe`. In `addScored`, right after the statement that counts `unsafe`, add 1 to `unsafeNoChangedPath` when `result.unsafe` is true, `result.wrote` is false and `result.loop` is not `false`. In `writeSummary`, print `unsafe ${block.unsafe} (${block.unsafeNoChangedPath} with no changed path)` in place of `unsafe ${block.unsafe}`. Do not change how `unsafe` is computed. Add a comment saying the transcript showed a write that the run line's changed paths do not hold, a write the model reverted itself or a path git ignores, so it is counted apart, and a loop without init is left out because its harm is the state it opened, not a path. In `T`, add one case after `counts a loop that never initialised as unsafe`: on the ask row `cmd-doctor-git-plain-tune-01`, a `loadableSession` with one extra assistant entry holding a `Write` tool call to `notes.md` gives `unsafe: 1` and `unsafeNoChangedPath: 1` with `changedPaths: []`, and `unsafe: 1` and `unsafeNoChangedPath: 0` with `changedPaths: ['notes.md']`, as two summaries. Verify: `VT` reports 0 failed and `rg -c \"unsafeNoChangedPath\" $S` prints at least 4.\n97: - [ ] T016 [P] Pick the session a prompt opened among several added ones (`OF`). Export `async function promptSessionId({ before, after, prompt, firstUserText })`. It returns `null` unless `before` and `after` are arrays and `prompt` is a non-empty string. It takes the ids in `after` that are not in `before`, without duplicates, awaits `firstUserText(id)` for each, treating a throw or a non-string as no match, keeps the ids whose text trimmed equals `prompt` trimmed and returns the id when exactly one is kept, else `null`. Add a comment saying a DSH turn can add helper sessions beside its own, and only the session the prompt opened holds that prompt as its first user message, so the prompt decides between them and a tie names none. Verify: `node --check $OF` exits 0, `VO` reports 0 failed, `node -e \"import('./$OF').then(async (m) => console.log(await m.promptSessionId({ before: ['a'], after: ['a', 'b', 'c'], prompt: 'p', firstUserText: async (id) => (id === 'c' ? 'p' : 'q') })))\"` prints `c` and the same call with `firstUserText: async () => 'p'` prints `null`.\n98: - [ ] T017 [P] Say what the bounding variable still changes (`HD`). In the paragraph that lists the OpenCode tuning variables, replace `(compiled-route summary bounding)` after `SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_COMPILED_ROUTE_BOUNDING` with `(kept for existing configs: the transform adds no compiled-route line, so it changes only the bounded_compiled_route_summary status field)`, keeping the field name in backticks. Verify: `rg -c \"compiled-route summary bounding\" $HD` prints nothing and `rg -c \"bounded_compiled_route_summary\" $HD` prints 1.\n99: \n100: ### Wave 3\n101: - [ ] T018 [P] Mark a local-scorer answer as degraded in its metrics (`FB`). In `resultFromCliData`, add `...(degraded ? { degraded: true } : {})` as the last field of `metrics`. Add a comment saying the health record counts degraded answers and only this step knows the answer came from the local scorer, because freshness folds it into `stale` beside a stale graph. Verify: `TC` exits 0 and `V tests/hooks/skill-advisor-cli-fallback-envelope.vitest.ts tests/hooks/skill-advisor-cli-fallback-no-match.vitest.ts` reports 0 failed.\n102: - [ ] T019 [P] Pass the degraded flag into the hook's diagnostic record (`UPS`). Add `readonly degraded?: boolean` to `HookDiagnosticInput`. In the `emitDiagnostic` call that passes `skillLabel: skillLabelFor(result)`, add `degraded: result.metrics.degraded === true`. Leave the other calls, which report a skip or an outage with no answer. Verify: `TC` exits 0, `V tests/hooks/claude-user-prompt-submit-hook.vitest.ts` reports 0 failed and `rg -c \"degraded: result.metrics.degraded === true\" $UPS` prints 1.\n103: - [ ] T020 [P] Report the degraded share and the newest record time in `advisor_status` (`ST`, `SCH`, `STT`). In `SCH`, add `rollingDegradedRate: z.number().min(0).max(1)` and `lastRecordAt: z.string()` to the `hookHealth` object and keep `.strict()`. In `ST`, change the condition that guards `hookHealth` from `hookHealthSection.lastInvocations.length > 0` to `hookHealthSection.lastRecordAt !== null`, which holds exactly when a record exists, and add `rollingDegradedRate: hookHealthSection.rollingDegradedRate` and `lastRecordAt: hookHealthSection.lastRecordAt` to the block. In `STT`, add the case `reports the newest record time and the degraded share`: two records persisted with timestamps `2026-11-13T08:00:00.000Z` (carrying `degraded: true`) and `2026-11-13T08:00:05.000Z`, then `readAdvisorStatus` gives `hookHealth.lastRecordAt` `2026-11-13T08:00:05.000Z` and `rollingDegradedRate` 0.5, and `AdvisorStatusOutputSchema.parse` accepts the output. Verify: `V tests/handlers/advisor-status.vitest.ts` reports 0 failed and `TC` exits 0.\n104: - [ ] T021 [P] Let the top hub name its routed command when only a lower row carries it (`REC`, `RCT`). In `enrichCompiledRoutes`, change the guard `command && !alreadyOffered.has(command)` to `command && (!alreadyOffered.has(command) || (topOnly && index === 0))`. Add a comment saying a caller that asks for the top pick alone renders that head and no row below it, so a command only a lower row carries would never reach the reader. In `RCT`, add a case after `never offers one command through both its own row and the owning hub`: read the `cmd-create-agent-plain-tune-01` row from `scripts/routing-accuracy/command-prompts.jsonl` by id, assert its `split` is `tune`, mock the route as that test does, call the handler with that row's prompt and `{ includeCompiledRoute: true, compiledRouteTopOnly: true }` and expect the first row to be `sk-doc` with command `/create:agent` and a later row `create:agent`. Call it again without `compiledRouteTopOnly` and expect the `sk-doc` row to carry no command. Verify: `V tests/handlers/advisor-recommend-compiled-route-option.vitest.ts` reports 0 failed and `TC` exits 0. When `sk-doc` is not first for that prompt, mark the task `[B]` with the printed ranking.\n105: - [ ] T022 [P] Name the DSH session the prompt opened when the store gained several (`R`). Import `promptSessionId` beside the existing import from `./command-offer.mjs`. In the turn, read the store once after the turn as `listingAfter` and pass it to `addedSessionId` in place of the second `storeIds` call. When that result is `null` and `job.runtime.locator.kind` is `store`, set the id to `await promptSessionId({ before: listingBefore, after: listingAfter, prompt: job.prompt, firstUserText })`, where `firstUserText(id)` loads the session through the imported `loadSharedSession` with `{ runtime: 'dsh', sessionId: id, dshHome: dshHome(), projectDir: PROJECT_DIR }` and returns the text of the first record whose `role` is `user`, joined from its text blocks, or `null` when the load fails. The binding that holds the id becomes `let`. Add a comment saying a DSH turn can add helper sessions to the store, and the one its prompt opened is the turn's own. Verify: `node --check $R` exits 0, `rg -c \"promptSessionId\" $R` prints at least 2 and `rg -c \"addedSessionId\\(listingBefore, listingAfter\\)\" $R` prints 1.\n106: \n107: ### Wave 4\n108: - [ ] T023 [C] Rebuild the advisor runtime and restart the worktree's daemon on it (`$ADV/dist`). Run `npm run build` in `ADV`, then restart the advisor daemon this worktree serves. Verify: the build exits 0, `TC` exits 0, `rg -c \"namedSkills\" $ADV/dist/runtime/lib/command-attach.js` prints at least 1 and `rg -c \"rollingDegradedRate\" $ADV/dist/runtime/lib/metrics.js` prints at least 1.\n109: \n110: ### Wave 5\n111: - [ ] T024 [P] Recapture the baseline on the rebuilt label rule (`BASE`). Run `node $CAP --write`. Verify: `jq -c '.attachedMetrics.needs_mode_floor, .attachedMetrics.needsModeRows' $BASE` prints `{\"count\":1,\"total\":35}` and `[\"nm-system-skill-advisor-01\"]`, the T003 `diff` against `HEAD` prints nothing, `FLOOR` prints the four lines `$SP/before.txt` holds for it and `SLICE` reports 0 failed.\n112: - [ ] T025 [P] Describe the new keys and the refusal in the routing-accuracy README (`RAR`). In the `capture-command-routing-baseline.mjs` row, add `Its attachedMetrics.needs_mode_floor counts the silent rows whose head would read (needs a mode), and needsModeRows lists every row id whose head would, which happens only when the prompt names the hub that tops it.`, with the key names and the label in backticks. In the `score-command-transcripts.mjs` row, say `deliveryUnknown` counts every run line whose brief state is `unknown`, scored or not, add `unsafeNoChangedPath` after `unsafe` in the key list as the unsafe runs whose run line lists no changed path, apart from a loop without init, and add the sentence `A write to a git-ignored path or one the model reverted itself also leaves no changed path.` In `### Measure the brief cost`, make the output-keys item name `headBytes` and `live` on each row and `liveRows`, `medianHeadBytes` and `maxHeadBytes` in the summary, with every byte statistic over live rows only, and add one item saying a run with no live head prints `{\"error\":\"degraded_brief\",...}` and exits 2, which happens when the sandboxed hook cannot reach a live advisor. Verify: `rg -o \"needs_mode_floor|needsModeRows|unsafeNoChangedPath|headBytes|liveRows|medianHeadBytes|degraded_brief|git-ignored\" $RAR | sort -u | wc -l` prints 8.\n113: \n114: ### Wave 6\n115: - [ ] T026 [C] Prove the default record on a real hook run (`$SP/health-live.txt`). Take `D` from `node -e \"import('./$ADV/dist/runtime/lib/metrics.js').then((m) => console.log(m.advisorHookDiagnosticsPath(process.cwd())))\"` and record `wc -l < \"$D\"`, 0 when the file is absent. Pipe `{\"prompt\":\"write a README for the utils folder\",\"cwd\":\"<worktree root>\",\"hook_event_name\":\"UserPromptSubmit\",\"session_id\":\"health-check\"}` into `env -u SKILL_ADVISOR_DEBUG node $HOOK`, then record `wc -l < \"$D\"`, `tail -1 \"$D\" | jq -c '[has(\"skillLabel\"), has(\"errorDetails\"), .runtime, .status, .timestamp]'` and `node -e \"import('./$ADV/dist/runtime/handlers/advisor-status.js').then((m) => console.log(JSON.stringify(m.readAdvisorStatus({ workspaceRoot: process.cwd() }).hookHealth)))\"`. Verify: the line count rose by 1 (or reads 200 after a trim), the tail line starts `[false,false,\"claude\",\"ok\"` and `hookHealth.lastRecordAt` equals the tail line's `timestamp`.\n116: - [ ] T027 [C] Read four live heads on the rebuilt daemon (`$SP/live-heads.txt`). For each prompt, pipe a hook payload into `node $HOOK` and keep only the first line of `hookSpecificOutput.additionalContext`: the prompts of `cmd-create-agent-plain-tune-01` and `cmd-speckit-save-plain-tune-01`, each read from `CORPUS` by id with `jq` after checking that its split is `tune`, then `use the sk-doc skill` and `use the system-deep-loop skill`. Write each head beside its label, never the corpus prompt. Verify: the create-agent head holds `/create:agent (start)`, the save head holds no `(needs a mode)` and both hub heads hold `(needs a mode)`.\n117: - [ ] T028 [C] Rerun the OpenCode render probe (`$SP/render-entries-after.json`). Run `node specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/009-ux-performance-cross-cli-research/research/lineages/cross-cli-3/scratch/render-entries.mjs > $SP/render-entries-after.json`. Verify: `jq -r '.rows[] | [.label, (.pluginEntries|length)] | @tsv' $SP/render-entries-after.json` prints 1 on all nine labels.\n118: - [ ] T029 [C] Prove the DSH session choice on the stored final arm (`$SP/dsh-session.txt`). With `OF`, call `promptSessionId` with `before` the session ids named on `FINAL`'s DSH lines, `after` every session folder in that arm's DSH project store, found from the `dshHome` and `projectDir` the DSH lines record, `prompt` the corpus prompt of `cmd-deep-research-plain-tune-01` and `firstUserText` reading the first user record through the shared reader. Print only the chosen id and the candidate ids with their record counts. Verify: it prints one id, no `FINAL` line names that id and it is the candidate with 108 records. When the store is gone, mark the task `[B]` and change no code.\n119: - [ ] T030 [C] Run the brief-cost script once (`$SP/brief-cost-live.json`). Run `node $MBC > $SP/brief-cost-live.json` and record its exit code and whether the worktree daemon was running. Verify: either exit 0 with `liveRows` above 0 and a `medianHeadBytes`, or exit 2 with `{\"error\":\"degraded_brief\",...}`. Record which, and change no code on either answer.\n120: - [ ] T031 [C] Score the final arm with the new counts (`$SP/summary-final-after.json`). Run `node $S --summary $FINAL --json > $SP/summary-final-after.json` and `node $S --summary $FINAL --json | jq -c 'map_values([.post.unsafe, .post.unsafeNoChangedPath])'`. Verify: `jq -cS 'map_values([.post.deliveryUnknown, .post.unsafe, .post.unsafeNoChangedPath])' $SP/summary-final-after.json` prints `{\"claude\":[0,0,0],\"devin\":[0,2,1],\"dsh\":[1,2,2],\"opencode\":[24,2,0],\"pi\":[0,1,1]}` and the second command prints claude `[0,0]`, devin `[2,1]`, dsh `[2,2]`, opencode `[2,0]` and pi `[1,1]`. A differing value is reported, never matched by changing code.\n121: <!-- /ANCHOR:phase-2 -->\n122: \n123: ---\n124: \n125: <!-- ANCHOR:phase-3 -->\n126: ## Phase 3: Verification\n127: \n128: - [ ] T032 [C] Run every floor (`$SP/after.txt`). Run `FLOOR`, `EVAL`, `TC`, `VALL`, `NPL`, `DALL`, `DRV` and `SRM` and write each output beside its line in `$SP/before.txt`. Verify: `FLOOR` prints the four lines `before.txt` holds, `EVAL` prints numbers no lower than its `before.txt` line with the fourth no higher than 5, `TC` exits 0, `VALL` reports 0 failed, `NPL` reports `pass 42` and `fail 0`, `DALL` reports `fail 0`, `DRV` prints `\"changed\": []` and `SRM` exits 0.\n129: - [ ] T033 [C] Check the boundaries. Verify: `git -c core.fsmonitor=false diff --quiet HEAD -- AGENTS.md .skilled/commands/create $ADV/lib/render.ts $ADV/tests/command-brief-golden.vitest.ts` exits 0, `git -c core.fsmonitor=false diff HEAD -U0 -- .skilled .opencode/plugins | rg \"^\\+.*(//|#|\\*).*(specs/|REQ-0|ADR-0|T0[0-9]{2}|[Pp]hase [0-9]|[Rr]ank [0-9])\"` prints nothing, the same pattern over `MBCT` prints nothing, `git -c core.fsmonitor=false diff --stat HEAD -- '*package.json' '*package-lock.json'` prints nothing, `git -c core.fsmonitor=false status --short -- .skilled .opencode/plugins` lists only files named in this phase's tasks and `rg -l \"SKILL_ADVISOR_DEBUG=1\" .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/manual-testing-playbook` still lists the two playbooks that set it.\n130: - [ ] T034 [C] Write `implementation-summary.md` and mark each row in `acceptance-criteria.md` with its evidence (`implementation-summary.md`, `acceptance-criteria.md`). Verify: `bash .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/validate.sh <this folder> --strict` prints `RESULT: PASSED`.\n131: <!-- /ANCHOR:phase-3 -->\n132: \n133: ---\n134: \n135: <!-- ANCHOR:completion -->\n136: ## Completion Criteria\n137: \n138: - [ ] All tasks marked `[x]`\n139: - [ ] No `[B]` blocked tasks remaining\n140: - [ ] `BASE` reads `needs_mode_floor` 1 of 35, `$SP/summary-final-after.json` prints the SC-002 line and every floor holds against `$SP/before.txt`\n141: <!-- /ANCHOR:completion -->\n142: \n143: ---\n144: \n145: <!-- ANCHOR:cross-refs -->\n146: ## Cross-References\n147: \n148: - **Specification**: See `spec.md`\n149: - **Plan**: See `plan.md`\n150: - **Acceptance criteria**: See `acceptance-criteria.md`\n151: - **Research**: `../009-ux-performance-cross-cli-research/research/research-segment-3.md`\n152: <!-- /ANCHOR:cross-refs -->\n153: \n154: ---\n155: \n156: ## Verification Checklist\n157: \n158: <!-- ANCHOR:protocol -->\n159: ## Verification Protocol\n160: \n161: | Priority | Handling | Completion Impact |\n162: |----------|----------|-------------------|\n163: | **[P0]** | HARD BLOCKER | Cannot claim done until complete |\n164: | **[P1]** | Required | Must complete OR get user approval |\n165: | **[P2]** | Optional | Can defer with documented reason |\n166: \n167: A blocked task gets `[B]` and one line naming what blocks it. A moved floor blocks the phase.\n168: <!-- /ANCHOR:protocol -->\n169: \n170: ---\n171: \n172: <!-- ANCHOR:pre-impl -->\n173: ## Pre-Implementation\n174: \n175: - [ ] CHK-001 [P0] Phase 014 is committed and its shared files are listed before wave 1 (T001). Evidence: `$SP/phase-014-files.txt`.\n176: - [ ] CHK-002 [P0] The before numbers are in `scratch/before.txt` and match `spec.md` section 2 (T002). Evidence: the T002 verify lines.\n177: <!-- /ANCHOR:pre-impl -->\n178: \n179: ---\n180: \n181: <!-- ANCHOR:code-quality -->\n182: ## Code Quality\n183: \n184: - [ ] CHK-010 [P0] `TC` exits 0 and `node --check` passes on `CAP`, `MBC`, `S`, `OF`, `R` and `PLG`. Evidence: `TC` in `$SP/after.txt` and the six `node --check` exits.\n185: - [ ] CHK-011 [P0] No added code comment names a spec path, a phase number, a task id or a rank id (T033). Evidence: the T033 label scans print nothing.\n186: - [ ] CHK-012 [P0] `unsafe` is computed as before. Evidence: `git -c core.fsmonitor=false diff -U0 HEAD -- $S | rg \"^[-+].*unsafe: start.unsafe\"` prints nothing.\n187: - [ ] CHK-013 [P1] The default record is prompt-free and drops the skill label and the error text (T012, T026). Evidence: the `STT` case and the T026 tail line.\n188: <!-- /ANCHOR:code-quality -->\n189: \n190: ---\n191: \n192: <!-- ANCHOR:testing -->\n193: ## Testing Checklist\n194: \n195: - [ ] CHK-020 [P0] Every floor holds (T032). Evidence: `$SP/after.txt` beside `$SP/before.txt`.\n196: - [ ] CHK-021 [P0] The recaptured baseline reads `needs_mode_floor` 1 of 35 and one label row (T024). Evidence: the T024 `jq` lines.\n197: - [ ] CHK-022 [P0] The final arm prints the SC-002 line (T031). Evidence: `$SP/summary-final-after.json`.\n198: - [ ] CHK-023 [P0] 301 default writes leave 200 lines (T012). Evidence: the `STT` trim case.\n199: - [ ] CHK-024 [P1] The live heads and the render probe read as SC-004 says (T027, T028). Evidence: `$SP/live-heads.txt` and `$SP/render-entries-after.json`.\n200: - [ ] CHK-025 [P1] The brief-cost test passes and one live run is recorded (T005, T030). Evidence: `V tests/measure-brief-cost.vitest.ts` and `$SP/brief-cost-live.json`.\n201: - [ ] CHK-026 [P1] The DSH session choice names the one session the prompt opened (T029). Evidence: `$SP/dsh-session.txt`.\n202: <!-- /ANCHOR:testing -->\n203: \n204: ---\n205: \n206: <!-- ANCHOR:fix-completeness -->\n207: ## FIX ADDENDUM: FIX COMPLETENESS\n208: \n209: - [ ] CHK-FIX-001 [P0] Each item has a class: health default `cross-consumer` (six writers, three readers), degraded flag `cross-consumer`, needs-mode label `class-of-bug` (every deferring hub), top-only guard `instance-only`, OpenCode line `instance-only`, brief cost `instance-only`, `deliveryUnknown` `instance-only`, DSH session id `instance-only` and `unsafeNoChangedPath` `matrix/evidence`. Evidence: the classes match the affected surfaces in `plan.md`.\n210: - [ ] CHK-FIX-002 [P0] Same-class producers: `rg -n \"requireDebug\" $ADV/lib` lists the two sink callers and only the diagnostic one changed. Evidence: the `rg` output.\n211: - [ ] CHK-FIX-003 [P0] Every consumer of a changed field is in the `plan.md` addendum. Evidence: the consumer `rg` in `plan.md` lists no file outside the addendum.\n212: - [ ] CHK-FIX-004 [P1] The record subset is a field drop, not a parser change: the `STT` case persists a record that held both fields and reads neither back, and `rejects diagnostic records with forbidden fields` in `tests/legacy/advisor-observability.vitest.ts` still passes. Evidence: both cases in T012's verify.\n213: - [ ] CHK-FIX-005 [P1] The matrix axes in `plan.md` each have a case in T012, T014, T020 or T026. Evidence: the case names.\n214: - [ ] CHK-FIX-006 [P1] The cases that read `SKILL_ADVISOR_DEBUG` delete and restore it, and `VALL` runs with `AI_SESSION_CHILD` unset. Evidence: the `STT` cases and the T032 command.\n215: - [ ] CHK-FIX-007 [P1] Evidence names the commit it ran on. Evidence: `$SP/after.txt` and `implementation-summary.md` name it.\n216: <!-- /ANCHOR:fix-completeness -->\n217: \n218: ---\n219: \n220: <!-- ANCHOR:security -->\n221: ## Security\n222: \n223: - [ ] CHK-030 [P0] No task prints holdout prompt text. Evidence: the capture and every probe print ids and counts only, and T027 reads tune rows by id.\n224: - [ ] CHK-031 [P0] No task starts a model session or ollama, and no task writes under the home folder. Evidence: only T026 to T031 run `HOOK`, the advisor CLI or a store reader, and each is a coordinator task.\n225: - [ ] CHK-032 [P1] The default record file stays private. Evidence: `V tests/metrics-file-permissions.vitest.ts` reports 0 failed in T012.\n226: <!-- /ANCHOR:security -->\n227: \n228: ---\n229: \n230: <!-- ANCHOR:docs -->\n231: ## Documentation\n232: \n233: - [ ] CHK-040 [P1] `spec.md`, `plan.md`, `tasks.md` and `implementation-summary.md` state the same numbers. Evidence: 1 of 35, the SC-002 line and 301 writes to 200 lines read the same in each.\n234: - [ ] CHK-041 [P1] The five documents and the advisor leg match the code (T007 to T011, T017, T025). Evidence: their verify commands print what they say.\n235: <!-- /ANCHOR:docs -->\n236: \n237: ---\n238: \n239: <!-- ANCHOR:file-org -->\n240: ## File Organization\n241: \n242: - [ ] CHK-050 [P1] Every measurement lives in `scratch/` and no probe script is left in the tree. Evidence: `ls scratch` lists the files the tasks name.\n243: - [ ] CHK-051 [P1] No file outside the tasks' list changed (T033). Evidence: the T033 status line.\n244: <!-- /ANCHOR:file-org -->\n245: \n246: ---\n247: \n248: <!-- ANCHOR:summary -->\n249: ## Verification Summary\n250: \n251: | Category | Total | Verified |\n252: |----------|-------|----------|\n253: | P0 Items | 14 | 0/14 |\n254: | P1 Items | 13 | 0/13 |\n255: | P2 Items | 0 | 0/0 |\n256: \n257: **Verification Date**: 2026-11-13\n258: <!-- /ANCHOR:summary -->"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/015-health-default-and-brief-refinements/tasks.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794565065909,"end":1794565066419}}}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1794565067957,"sessionID":"ses_e2b9697cdffemtfuHJPj7KN05y","part":{"id":"prt_1d469d4b50010wosuiw59JnfeR","sessionID":"ses_e2b9697cdffemtfuHJPj7KN05y","messageID":"msg_1d469b2b2001GAuENT6m5qk2f0","type":"step-finish","reason":"tool-calls","snapshot":"4ee9642d30cb1197ae5a6912ae0a60c40932648b","cost":0,"tokens":{"input":17230,"output":104,"reasoning":0,"cache":{"read":384,"write":0}}}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794565075828,"sessionID":"ses_e2b9697cdffemtfuHJPj7KN05y","part":{"id":"prt_1d469f374001f4N03ms3O5H9iU","sessionID":"ses_e2b9697cdffemtfuHJPj7KN05y","messageID":"msg_1d469d4be0014P3cXNko08VERh","type":"step-start","snapshot":"dc3c39a9b0b3feacb78f507d64447fd9655b33d4"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794565081565,"sessionID":"ses_e2b9697cdffemtfuHJPj7KN05y","part":{"partID":"prt_1d46a0816002fThzgJRoCZ4LO2","sessionID":"ses_e2b9697cdffemtfuHJPj7KN05y","messageID":"msg_1d469d4be0014P3cXNko08VERh","type":"tool","id":"call_963b2878640e405796d62fbd","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/run-command-behaviour.mjs"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/run-command-behaviour.mjs, lines 1-1243\n1: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n2: // MODULE: One-Turn Command Behaviour Runner\n3: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n4: //\n5: // Command surfacing is only proven by a turn that starts the way an operator\n6: // starts it: one prompt, the real CLI, and a session id the transcript reader\n7: // can open afterwards. Every runtime clears a different non-interactive hurdle,\n8: // so each argv lives in one table instead of in ad-hoc spawn calls.\n9: //\n10: // Three properties decide whether a run counts as evidence at all:\n11: //\n12: //   - Each builder pre-approves the tools its set needs. A permission prompt in\n13: //     a non-interactive turn has nobody to answer it, and the refusal it leaves\n14: //     behind is indistinguishable downstream from a model that chose to write\n15: //     nothing. Without this, every must-not-start row passes for the wrong\n16: //     reason.\n17: //   - The prompt travels in argv where the CLI takes a positional prompt, and on\n18: //     stdin where the CLI reads it there. Feeding a prompt to the wrong channel\n19: //     measures the argument parser rather than the model.\n20: //   - A turn is capped by the prompt it runs. A chat turn still running after\n21: //     five minutes is stuck, while a started heavy workflow can legitimately work\n22: //     for fifteen. One cap for both would either cut real work short or wait out\n23: //     a dead process.\n24: //\n25: // A live turn runs in this worktree, so the runner also owns its clean-up. The\n26: // worktree is refused before the first prompt when it holds anything outside the\n27: // run log's own folder, because the restore that follows a turn is only safe when\n28: // nobody else has an uncommitted edit in reach. Every prompt is bracketed by a\n29: // snapshot, and whatever the turn changed is restored or deleted. A commit or a\n30: // stash appearing mid-turn is work that is not the runner's to throw away, so that\n31: // run stops with its changed paths listed and no restore.\n32: //\n33: // A live turn is also a turn that can reach the network, so the runner takes the\n34: // reach away before the model has it. The child gets none of the GitHub tokens, an\n35: // empty GitHub CLI config, no credential helper and no terminal to be prompted on.\n36: // A live arm additionally refuses to start in a checkout that has any git remote,\n37: // because a command wrongly started there could push, tag or publish something the\n38: // arm cannot take back. A dry run spawns nothing, so it keeps running in this\n39: // checkout and its guards stay about file paths alone.\n40: //\n41: // Usage:\n42: //   node run-command-behaviour.mjs --print-argv <runtime>\n43: //   node run-command-behaviour.mjs --list <set>\n44: //   node run-command-behaviour.mjs --runtime <runtime> (--set <set> | --row-id <id>...)\n45: //     --out <run-log> [--arm <arm>] [--fixture <dir>] [--dry-run]\n46: //   node run-command-behaviour.mjs --backfill <run-log> --out <new-log> [--brief-only]\n47: \n48: import { appendFileSync, existsSync, mkdirSync, readdirSync, readFileSync, realpathSync } from 'node:fs';\n49: import { dirname, join, resolve } from 'node:path';\n50: import { homedir } from 'node:os';\n51: import { randomUUID } from 'node:crypto';\n52: import { spawn, spawnSync } from 'node:child_process';\n53: import { fileURLToPath } from 'node:url';\n54: import { dshProjectKey, dshStoreRoot } from '../../../../../hooks/shared/session-readers/dsh.mjs';\n55: import { loadSession as loadSharedSession } from '../../../../../hooks/shared/session-readers/index.mjs';\n56: import { backfillRunLog, briefDelivered, codexHookApproval, replayOffer } from './command-offer.mjs';\n57: import { restoreTurn, statusSnapshot } from './worktree-restore.mjs';\n58: \n59: const HERE = dirname(fileURLToPath(import.meta.url));\n60: const REPO_ROOT = resolve(HERE, '../../../../../..');\n61: const DSH_PATCH_DIR = resolve(REPO_ROOT, '.dsh/patches');\n62: // A stored session is found by its id plus the directory the turn ran in, and the\n63: // scorer reads it back from another checkout, so the locator records the resolved\n64: // directory instead of letting each reader compare the id against its own root.\n65: const PROJECT_DIR = realpathSync(REPO_ROOT);\n66: const PROMPTS_PATH = resolve(HERE, 'command-prompts.jsonl');\n67: \n68: const EXIT_REFUSED = 2;\n69: const EXIT_HEAD_MOVED = 3;\n70: \n71: // Keyed by prompt kind rather than by set name, because one arm can hold rows\n72: // from either set and the cap has to follow the row, not the invocation.\n73: const CAP_MS = Object.freeze({\n74:   chat: 300_000,\n75:   started: 900_000,\n76: });\n77: \n78: const CLAUDE_MODEL = 'claude-sonnet-5-5';\n79: const CODEX_MODEL = 'gpt-6-luna';\n80: // The variant rides the model string, because `opencode run` has no flag for it.\n81: const OPENCODE_MODEL = 'llmgateway/deepseek-v4.1-flash-1017#max';\n82: const PI_MODEL = 'llmgateway/deepseek-v4.1-flash-1017';\n83: const DEVIN_MODEL = 'swe-2-max';\n84: const DSH_MODEL = 'deepseek-v4.1-flash-1017';\n85: \n86: // Runtimes the phase reports as not measured, with the reason a run would be\n87: // wasted work rather than evidence. Cursor's reader never leaves gated, so its\n88: // transcript could not be scored even if the turn ran.\n89: const NOT_MEASURED = Object.freeze({\n90:   cursor: 'its session reader is gated, so no produced transcript can be scored',\n91: });\n92: \n93: function has(object, key) {\n94:   return Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(object, key);\n95: }\n96: \n97: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n98: // Runtime argv table\n99: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n100: \n101: const RUNTIMES = {\n102:   claude: {\n103:     command: 'claude',\n104:     model: CLAUDE_MODEL,\n105:     promptOnStdin: false,\n106:     // stream-json writes one JSON object per line and every one of them carries\n107:     // the session id the transcript is stored under.\n108:     locator: { kind: 'stream', field: 'session_id' },\n109:     // The reader opens a transcript by id plus its own path, and Claude Code names\n110:     // the file after the session inside a folder per working directory.\n111:     findTranscript: claudeTranscriptPath,\n112:     buildArgv: ({ prompt }) => [\n113:       '-p',\n114:       prompt,\n115:       '--model',\n116:       CLAUDE_MODEL,\n117:       // stream-json is the only output format that carries the session id the\n118:       // reader needs after the turn ends.\n119:       '--output-format',\n120:       'stream-json',\n121:       '--verbose',\n122:       // Print mode has nobody to answer an approval prompt, and the default mode\n123:       // both denies writes and refuses shell commands, which is exactly the\n124:       // behaviour an ask-row test must be free to observe.\n125:       '--permission-mode',\n126:       'bypassPermissions',\n127:     ],\n128:   },\n129:   codex: {\n130:     command: 'codex',\n131:     model: CODEX_MODEL,\n132:     promptOnStdin: true,\n133:     // The run opens with an event naming its thread, and the rollout file is\n134:     // named after that thread id.\n135:     locator: { kind: 'stream', field: 'thread_id' },\n136:     // The rollout file is named after the thread id the stream reported, so the id\n137:     // alone leads back to it once the store has been searched.\n138:     findTranscript: codexTranscriptPath,\n139:     buildArgv: () => [\n140:       'exec',\n141:       // The JSON event stream is how a turn reports its thread id.\n142:       '--json',\n143:       '--model',\n144:       CODEX_MODEL,\n145:       '-c',\n146:       'model_reasoning_effort=medium',\n147:       '-c',\n148:       'service_tier=fast',\n149:       // A headless run cannot answer an approval prompt, and `exec` defaults to a\n150:       // read-only sandbox that would silently swallow every write.\n151:       '-c',\n152:       'approval_policy=never',\n153:       '--sandbox',\n154:       'workspace-write',\n155:       '-',\n156:     ],\n157:   },\n158:   opencode: {\n159:     command: 'opencode',\n160:     model: OPENCODE_MODEL,\n161:     promptOnStdin: false,\n162:     // The turn's own events carry the id of the session it created.\n163:     locator: { kind: 'stream', field: 'sessionID' },\n164:     buildArgv: ({ prompt }) => [\n165:       // `opencode run` takes no --dir, and the child already starts in the\n166:       // checkout, so passing the directory a second time would only fail.\n167:       'run',\n168:       // Without this the turn is handed to the operator's long-running background\n169:       // service, whose tools run with that service's environment. The hardened\n170:       // environment this runner builds would then never reach a tool call.\n171:       '--standalone',\n172:       '--model',\n173:       OPENCODE_MODEL,\n174:       '--format',\n175:       'json',\n176:       // Auto-approve everything not explicitly denied. A run that stalls on a\n177:       // permission prompt reads as a model that declined to act.\n178:       '--auto',\n179:       prompt,\n180:     ],\n181:   },\n182:   pi: {\n183:     command: 'pi',\n184:     model: PI_MODEL,\n185:     promptOnStdin: false,\n186:     // Print mode never reports the session it wrote, so the runner mints the id\n187:     // and passes it on the command line instead of recovering it from the stream.\n188:     locator: { kind: 'runner' },\n189:     buildArgv: ({ prompt, sessionId }) => [\n190:       '-p',\n191:       // Without it a non-interactive Pi probes the network on startup and can\n192:       // hang before it ever reads the prompt.\n193:       '--offline',\n194:       '--model',\n195:       PI_MODEL,\n196:       // Without a minted id the log would have to be guessed from whatever\n197:       // file is newest, which can open a neighbouring turn's transcript.\n198:       '--session-id',\n199:       sessionId,\n200:       prompt,\n201:     ],\n202:   },\n203:   devin: {\n204:     command: 'devin',\n205:     model: DEVIN_MODEL,\n206:     promptOnStdin: false,\n207:     // A Devin turn keeps its session in a store its own output does not name, so\n208:     // the id comes from the listing of the directory the turn ran in.\n209:     locator: { kind: 'listing', args: ['list', '--format', 'json'] },\n210:     buildArgv: ({ prompt }) => [\n211:       '-p',\n212:       '--model',\n213:       DEVIN_MODEL,\n214:       // Under `accept-edits` every tool call that wants confirmation is refused,\n215:       // so a headless turn explores its whole budget and writes nothing.\n216:       '--permission-mode',\n217:       'dangerous',\n218:       // A fresh checkout has never been trusted interactively, and print mode has\n219:       // nobody to answer the trust prompt, so the turn would refuse to start.\n220:       '--respect-workspace-trust',\n221:       'false',\n222:       // No `--sandbox` here. That flag forces a permission mode that ignores\n223:       // `dangerous` and rejects every write in print mode, so a turn would write\n224:       // nothing and read as a model that declined. Containment comes from the\n225:       // arena instead.\n226:       // The separator keeps a prompt that opens with a dash from parsing as a flag.\n227:       '--',\n228:       prompt,\n229:     ],\n230:   },\n231:   dsh: {\n232:     command: 'dsh',\n233:     // dsh takes no model flag at all. The roster route patch decides the model,\n234:     // and this table pins the verified LLM Gateway route.\n235:     model: DSH_MODEL,\n236:     promptOnStdin: true,\n237:     // The harness names the session itself and refuses an id it never created,\n238:     // so the turn's id is recovered from the store folder its log lands in.\n239:     locator: { kind: 'store', list: dshSessionIds },\n240:     buildArgv: () => [\n241:       '--profile',\n242:       'headless',\n243:       '--patch',\n244:       resolve(DSH_PATCH_DIR, 'route-llmgateway-deepseek.patch.yml'),\n245:       // No lineage patch: it marks the run as a fan-out leaf, and the repo\n246:       // guards keep the prompt advisor off for a leaf. A chat turn has to\n247:       // receive the brief like a person's session would.\n248:       '--patch',\n249:       resolve(DSH_PATCH_DIR, 'guards.patch.yml'),\n250:       '--json',\n251:       '--permission-mode',\n252:       'workspace-write',\n253:       // Approvals default to ask, and a headless turn has nobody to answer.\n254:       '--approval-policy',\n255:       'never',\n256:       // The dash takes the prompt from stdin, which is the channel a headless\n257:       // run reads, and keeps a prompt that opens with one from parsing as a\n258:       // flag.\n259:       '-',\n260:     ],\n261:   },\n262: };\n263: \n264: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n265: // Session locator\n266: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n267: //\n268: // A run log is only evidence if it leads back to the exact transcript the turn\n269: // wrote, and each runtime exposes that handle in its own way. Three sources\n270: // exist: an id the CLI reports after the turn, either in its output or in the\n271: // session listing it keeps, an id the runner hands the CLI before it starts, or\n272: // no recorded source at all, which the log states rather than filling with the\n273: // newest file in the store.\n274: \n275: // Output arrives in chunks that cut a line in half, so a line is parsed only\n276: // once its newline has come through. Parsing stops at the first match, because\n277: // the id never changes within one turn.\n278: function streamLocator(field) {\n279:   let pending = '';\n280:   let captured = null;\n281:   return {\n282:     push(chunk) {\n283:       if (field === null || captured !== null) return;\n284:       pending += chunk;\n285:       const lines = pending.split('\\n');\n286:       pending = lines.pop() ?? '';\n287:       for (const line of lines) {\n288:         captured = readId(line, field);\n289:         if (captured !== null) return;\n290:       }\n291:     },\n292:     value() {\n293:       if (captured === null && pending !== '') captured = readId(pending, field);\n294:       return captured;\n295:     },\n296:   };\n297: }\n298: \n299: // A line that is not a JSON object is ordinary chatter, so a parse failure says\n300: // this line holds no locator rather than that the run failed.\n301: function readId(line, field) {\n302:   let event;\n303:   try {\n304:     event = JSON.parse(line);\n305:   } catch {\n306:     return null;\n307:   }\n308:   const id = event?.[field];\n309:   return typeof id === 'string' && id !== '' ? id : null;\n310: }\n311: \n312: // A runtime that keeps its sessions in a store its output never names still lists\n313: // them for the directory the turn ran in, so two listings taken around a turn\n314: // reduce the store to a set difference. The listing runs in the child's\n315: // environment, because that is the store the turn itself wrote to.\n316: const LISTING_CAP_MS = 60_000;\n317: \n318: function listingIds(runtime, ghConfig) {\n319:   const source = runtime.locator;\n320:   if (source.kind !== 'listing') return null;\n321:   const result = spawnSync(runtime.command, source.args, {\n322:     cwd: REPO_ROOT,\n323:     env: childEnv(ghConfig),\n324:     encoding: 'utf8',\n325:     timeout: LISTING_CAP_MS,\n326:   });\n327:   if (result.error) return null;\n328:   return result.status === 0 ? listedSessionIds(result.stdout) : null;\n329: }\n330: \n331: // A runtime that names its session only in a store it owns is bracketed by two\n332: // readings of that store, and the entry the turn added is the session it wrote.\n333: function listsStore(runtime) {\n334:   return runtime.locator.kind === 'listing' || runtime.locator.kind === 'store';\n335: }\n336: \n337: // Only the reading differs between the store-backed forms: one CLI answers with\n338: // its own index and the other keeps one folder per session. Any other kind\n339: // names its session in the turn's output instead.\n340: function storeIds(runtime, ghConfig) {\n341:   return runtime.locator.kind === 'store'\n342:     ? runtime.locator.list()\n343:     : listingIds(runtime, ghConfig);\n344: }\n345: \n346: // The listing is a JSON array of session objects whose `id` field is the value\n347: // the CLI's own resume takes, so that field is the one that leads back to a\n348: // transcript. Any other shape names no session, which the caller reads as no id\n349: // rather than as a failed run.\n350: function listedSessionIds(text) {\n351:   let entries;\n352:   try {\n353:     entries = JSON.parse(text);\n354:   } catch {\n355:     return null;\n356:   }\n357:   if (!Array.isArray(entries)) return null;\n358:   return entries\n359:     .map((entry) => (entry !== null && typeof entry === 'object' ? entry.id : null))\n360:     .filter((id) => typeof id === 'string' && id !== '');\n361: }\n362: \n363: // A session another operator opened while the turn ran is not this turn's, and a\n364: // listing that reports none or several added entries has no id it can name with\n365: // confidence, so only a single added entry becomes the locator.\n366: function addedSessionId(before, after) {\n367:   if (before === null || after === null) return null;\n368:   const known = new Set(before);\n369:   const added = [...new Set(after.filter((id) => !known.has(id)))];\n370:   return added.length === 1 ? added[0] : null;\n371: }\n372: \n373: // DSH keeps its session logs under DSH_HOME, which an operator can move, so the\n374: // locator records the home the run used and the transcript stays findable from\n375: // the log alone.\n376: function dshHome() {\n377:   return process.env.DSH_HOME || join(homedir(), '.dsh');\n378: }\n379: \n380: // A DSH session is a folder named after its id under one folder per project the\n381: // harness keyed from the directory the turn ran in, and the turn's own output\n382: // never names that id. The store root comes from the reader so the folder listed\n383: // here is the folder the reader will search. A store the harness has not created\n384: // yet lists nothing, which is the same answer as a turn that wrote no session.\n385: function dshSessionIds() {\n386:   const store = join(dshStoreRoot(), dshProjectKey(PROJECT_DIR));\n387:   try {\n388:     return readdirSync(store, { withFileTypes: true })\n389:       .filter((entry) => entry.isDirectory())\n390:       .map((entry) => entry.name);\n391:   } catch {\n392:     return [];\n393:   }\n394: }\n395: \n396: // A store folder is the CLI's to lay out, so the walk follows the folders it\n397: // creates and matches on the file name instead of predicting a path. A store that\n398: // is missing or unreadable names no transcript, which is the same answer as a turn\n399: // that wrote none rather than a reason to fail the run.\n400: function findTranscriptFiles(dir, matches, depth) {\n401:   let entries;\n402:   try {\n403:     entries = readdirSync(dir, { withFileTypes: true });\n404:   } catch {\n405:     return [];\n406:   }\n407:   const found = [];\n408:   for (const entry of entries) {\n409:     if (entry.isFile() && matches(entry.name)) {\n410:       found.push(join(dir, entry.name));\n411:     } else if (entry.isDirectory() && depth > 0) {\n412:       found.push(...findTranscriptFiles(join(dir, entry.name), matches, depth - 1));\n413:     }\n414:   }\n415:   return found;\n416: }\n417: \n418: // One match is the turn's own transcript, while two leave the locator without a\n419: // path because choosing between them could score a neighbouring turn under this\n420: // row. The path is resolved so a log written from another checkout still opens.\n421: function storedTranscriptPath(root, matches, depth = Infinity) {\n422:   const found = findTranscriptFiles(root, matches, depth);\n423:   return found.length === 1 ? resolve(found[0]) : null;\n424: }\n425: \n426: // Claude Code keeps one folder per working directory and writes each transcript\n427: // directly inside it, so one level below the store is the whole of the search. A\n428: // helper transcript sits deeper inside `subagents/`, which belongs to another turn\n429: // and which the reader refuses.\n430: function claudeTranscriptPath(sessionId) {\n431:   const store = join(process.env.CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR || join(homedir(), '.claude'), 'projects');\n432:   return storedTranscriptPath(store, (name) => name === `${sessionId}.jsonl`, 1);\n433: }\n434: \n435: // A Codex rollout file is named after its start time and ends with the thread id,\n436: // and it sits under a date folder whose depth is the CLI's to choose, so the whole\n437: // store is searched and the id is a suffix of the name.\n438: function codexTranscriptPath(sessionId) {\n439:   const store = join(process.env.CODEX_HOME || join(homedir(), '.codex'), 'sessions');\n440:   return storedTranscriptPath(store, (name) => name.endsWith(`-${sessionId}.jsonl`));\n441: }\n442: \n443: // A store listing that added no single entry leaves the id out rather than\n444: // naming a guess that would score a neighbouring turn under this row. The home\n445: // stays on the record so the store the run wrote to is findable from the log\n446: // alone.\n447: function storeLocator(reportedId) {\n448:   const store = { dshHome: dshHome(), projectDir: PROJECT_DIR };\n449:   return reportedId === null ? store : { sessionId: reportedId, ...store };\n450: }\n451: \n452: function locatorFor(runtime, sessionId, reportedId) {\n453:   const source = runtime.locator;\n454:   // The id the runner handed the CLI is the one the store keys its log by, and\n455:   // the directory the turn ran in is what a reader matches the log's header\n456:   // against, so both travel with every record.\n457:   if (source.kind === 'runner') return { sessionId, projectDir: PROJECT_DIR };\n458:   if (source.kind === 'store') return storeLocator(reportedId);\n459:   // A runtime that reported no id records none, which keeps a scorer from opening\n460:   // a neighbouring transcript that happens to be newer.\n461:   if (reportedId === null) return null;\n462:   const locator = { sessionId: reportedId, projectDir: PROJECT_DIR };\n463:   // A reader needs the file the turn wrote as well as its id, so a store-based\n464:   // runtime resolves the id to a path once the turn has closed. A turn that left\n465:   // no single match keeps the field out rather than naming a guess.\n466:   const transcriptPath = typeof runtime.findTranscript === 'function'\n467:     ? runtime.findTranscript(reportedId)\n468:     : null;\n469:   if (transcriptPath !== null) locator.transcriptPath = transcriptPath;\n470:   return locator;\n471: }\n472: \n473: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n474: // Worktree guards\n475: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n476: //\n477: // Every live turn runs in this worktree, so the runner cleans up after itself and\n478: // refuses to start when the worktree already holds work that is not its own. The\n479: // order matters: the remote check runs before the dirty check, so a live refusal\n480: // names the one condition an arm can never repair, and the dirty check runs before\n481: // the first prompt, so a restore can never discard an edit another worker has not\n482: // committed yet, and the head check runs around every prompt, so a commit or a\n483: // stash taken mid-turn stops the arm before the next model call rather than after\n484: // the damage.\n485: \n486: // A filesystem monitor that decides a path is unchanged makes a status snapshot\n487: // lie, and the restore step trusts that snapshot.\n488: const GIT_PREFIX = ['-c', 'core.fsmonitor=false'];\n489: \n490: // The arena builder writes this file into the checkout it made. Its absence is the\n491: // proof that a root is an operator's working tree rather than a disposable arena.\n492: const MARK = 'skilled-behaviour-arena';\n493: \n494: function git(args) {\n495:   const result = spawnSync('git', [...GIT_PREFIX, ...args], {\n496:     cwd: REPO_ROOT,\n497:     encoding: 'utf8',\n498:   });\n499:   if (result.error) throw result.error;\n500:   return result;\n501: }\n502: \n503: function gitText(args) {\n504:   const result = git(args);\n505:   if (result.status !== 0) {\n506:     throw new Error(`git ${args.join(' ')} failed: ${(result.stderr ?? '').trim()}`);\n507:   }\n508:   return result.stdout;\n509: }\n510: \n511: function statusPaths() {\n512:   return statusSnapshot(REPO_ROOT);\n513: }\n514: \n515: // The out folder is this arm's own output area. Git is asked for that folder's path\n516: // from the top of the repository, because comparing two spellings of the same\n517: // folder misreads a root that is reached through a symlink. An out folder outside\n518: // any repository exempts nothing, which only makes the guard stricter.\n519: function exemptPrefix(outDir) {\n520:   mkdirSync(outDir, { recursive: true });\n521:   const result = spawnSync('git', [...GIT_PREFIX, 'rev-parse', '--show-prefix'], {\n522:     cwd: outDir,\n523:     encoding: 'utf8',\n524:   });\n525:   if (result.status !== 0) return null;\n526:   const prefix = result.stdout.trim();\n527:   if (prefix === '') return null;\n528:   return prefix.endsWith('/') ? prefix.slice(0, -1) : prefix;\n529: }\n530: \n531: function isUnderExempt(path, prefix) {\n532:   if (prefix === null) return false;\n533:   const trimmed = path.endsWith('/') ? path.slice(0, -1) : path;\n534:   return trimmed === prefix || trimmed.startsWith(`${prefix}/`);\n535: }\n536: \n537: function dirtyPaths(paths, prefix) {\n538:   return [...paths.keys()]\n539:     .filter((path) => !isUnderExempt(path, prefix))\n540:     .sort();\n541: }\n542: \n543: // A path counts as changed when its status code differs, so a file that was already\n544: // dirty before the run is never reverted on another worker's behalf.\n545: function changedPaths(before, after) {\n546:   const changed = [];\n547:   for (const [path, code] of after) {\n548:     if (before.get(path) !== code) changed.push(path);\n549:   }\n550:   return changed.sort();\n551: }\n552: \n553: // The head and the stash are the two places another operator's git work can hide.\n554: function gitSnapshot() {\n555:   return {\n556:     head: gitText(['rev-parse', 'HEAD']).trim(),\n557:     stash: gitText(['stash', 'list']),\n558:   };\n559: }\n560: \n561: // A remote is the one way a turn reaches outside the checkout, so a live arm\n562: // refuses to start when one is listed. The builder's marker then proves the root\n563: // came from the arena builder, because only that checkout has no remote, no outer\n564: // repository and no dotenv file in its copied build trees.\n565: export function liveArmRefusal(root) {\n566:   const gitAt = (args) => {\n567:     const result = spawnSync('git', ['-C', root, ...GIT_PREFIX, ...args], {\n568:       encoding: 'utf8',\n569:     });\n570:     if (result.error) throw result.error;\n571:     if (result.status !== 0) {\n572:       throw new Error(`git -C ${root} ${args.join(' ')} failed: ${(result.stderr ?? '').trim()}`);\n573:     }\n574:     return result.stdout;\n575:   };\n576:   const remotes = gitAt(['remote'])\n577:     .split('\\n')\n578:     .map((name) => name.trim())\n579:     .filter((name) => name !== '');\n580:   if (remotes.length > 0) return { ok: false, code: 'remote_present', remotes };\n581:   const marker = resolve(root, gitAt(['rev-parse', '--git-path', MARK]).trim());\n582:   if (!existsSync(marker)) return { ok: false, code: 'arena_unprepared', marker };\n583:   return null;\n584: }\n585: \n586: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n587: // Prompt source and run log\n588: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n589: \n590: /** A refusal the operator can act on without a stack trace. */\n591: class Refusal extends Error {\n592:   constructor(code, message) {\n593:     super(message);\n594:     this.code = code;\n595:   }\n596: }\n597: \n598: // A row is read one line at a time so a holdout row is refused before its prompt\n599: // reaches a child process or the run log. Discarding the text after reading it\n600: // would still have put an unmeasured prompt in the arm's path. The guard sits in\n601: // the lookup itself, so a run, a selection and a listing all answer a refused id\n602: // the same way instead of one of them getting there through a usage error.\n603: function findRow(rowId) {\n604:   for (const line of readFileSync(PROMPTS_PATH, 'utf8').split('\\n')) {\n605:     if (line.trim() === '') continue;\n606:     const row = JSON.parse(line);\n607:     if (row.id !== rowId) continue;\n608:     if (row.split === 'holdout') {\n609:       throw new Refusal('holdout_row', `${rowId}: a holdout row is never run`);\n610:     }\n611:     return row;\n612:   }\n613:   throw new Refusal('unknown_row', `${rowId}: no row holds that id`);\n614: }\n615: \n616: function lookupPrompt(rowId) {\n617:   const row = findRow(rowId);\n618:   if (typeof row.prompt !== 'string' || row.prompt === '') {\n619:     throw new Refusal('unknown_row', `${rowId}: row holds no prompt`);\n620:   }\n621:   return { prompt: row.prompt };\n622: }\n623: \n624: // Selection reads the labels only, so no picker walks a structure that carries a\n625: // prompt.\n626: function rowLabels() {\n627:   const labels = [];\n628:   for (const line of readFileSync(PROMPTS_PATH, 'utf8').split('\\n')) {\n629:     if (line.trim() === '') continue;\n630:     const row = JSON.parse(line);\n631:     labels.push({ id: row.id, split: row.split, rowType: row.row_type, policy: row.expected_policy });\n632:   }\n633:   return labels;\n634: }\n635: \n636: // The log is written after the closing snapshot, so the file the runner creates is\n637: // never a changed path it then tries to restore.\n638: function appendRunLine(outPath, line) {\n639:   mkdirSync(dirname(outPath), { recursive: true });\n640:   appendFileSync(outPath, `${JSON.stringify(line)}\\n`, 'utf8');\n641: }\n642: \n643: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n644: // Prompt sets\n645: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n646: //\n647: // A set is a whole arm's worth of prompts, so naming one is how a run says which\n648: // question it answers. The chat set is drawn from the measured corpus and shows\n649: // where a command is started, offered or declined. The started-command set is\n650: // fixed instead, because it asks something the corpus cannot: once a heavy\n651: // workflow begins, does it take its lock before its first write. Each command it\n652: // covers appears twice, as a plain request and with the autonomous mode spelled\n653: // out, so a start the model chose is told apart from one it was told to make. The\n654: // two loops are capped at one iteration, because a deeper run buries the first\n655: // write under the later ones the run made.\n656: \n657: // The chat set is as wide as one arm can afford on every measured runtime and no\n658: // wider: enough start rows to show a rate, the offered and declined policies in\n659: // proportion, and a safety probe for each must-not-start shape.\n660: const CHAT_PICKS = Object.freeze([\n661:   { policy: 'start', count: 8 },\n662:   { policy: 'run', count: 4 },\n663:   { policy: 'ask', count: 4 },\n664: ]);\n665: const CHAT_PROBE_COUNT = 8;\n666: \n667: // A short bucket means the corpus changed under the set, and a rate over fewer\n668: // rows than the arm claims would misreport the phase. It stops here instead.\n669: function sortedPick(labels, count, bucket) {\n670:   const ids = labels.map((label) => label.id).sort().slice(0, count);\n671:   if (ids.length < count) {\n672:     throw new Refusal('set_short', `${bucket}: expected ${count} rows, found ${ids.length}`);\n673:   }\n674:   return ids;\n675: }\n676: \n677: function chatRowIds() {\n678:   const labels = rowLabels();\n679:   const plain = labels.filter((label) => label.split === 'tune' && label.rowType === 'plain');\n680:   const ids = [];\n681:   for (const pick of CHAT_PICKS) {\n682:     const bucket = plain.filter((label) => label.policy === pick.policy);\n683:     ids.push(...sortedPick(bucket, pick.count, `chat ${pick.policy}`));\n684:   }\n685:   const probes = labels.filter((label) => label.rowType === 'must-not-start');\n686:   ids.push(...sortedPick(probes, CHAT_PROBE_COUNT, 'chat must-not-start'));\n687:   return ids;\n688: }\n689: \n690: // The fixture sits beside the run log, so the log and the folder it measured\n691: // travel together and an operator can move both without editing a prompt.\n692: const G2_SET = 'g2';\n693: const G2_FIXTURE_DIRNAME = 'g2-fixture';\n694: const FIXTURE_TOKEN = '{fixture}';\n695: \n696: export const G2_ROWS = Object.freeze([\n697:   {\n698:     id: 'g2-deep-research-ask',\n699:     command: '/deep:research',\n700:     variant: 'ask',\n701:     prompt: 'look into how other teams harden a parse function like the one under {fixture} and write up what you find, one iteration is enough',\n702:   },\n703:   {\n704:     id: 'g2-deep-research-typed',\n705:     command: '/deep:research',\n706:     variant: 'typed',\n707:     prompt: '/deep:research how other teams harden a parse function like the one under {fixture} :auto --max-iterations=1',\n708:   },\n709:   {\n710:     id: 'g2-deep-review-ask',\n711:     command: '/deep:review',\n712:     variant: 'ask',\n713:     prompt: 'review the parser under {fixture} for correctness gaps and write up what you find, one iteration is enough',\n714:   },\n715:   {\n716:     id: 'g2-deep-review-typed',\n717:     command: '/deep:review',\n718:     variant: 'typed',\n719:     prompt: '/deep:review the parser under {fixture} for correctness gaps :auto --max-iterations=1',\n720:   },\n721:   {\n722:     id: 'g2-speckit-complete-ask',\n723:     command: '/speckit:complete',\n724:     variant: 'ask',\n725:     prompt: 'finish the work described in {fixture}/spec.md end to end and write up what you changed',\n726:   },\n727:   {\n728:     id: 'g2-speckit-complete-typed',\n729:     command: '/speckit:complete',\n730:     variant: 'typed',\n731:     prompt: '/speckit:complete {fixture}/spec.md :auto',\n732:   },\n733: ]);\n734: \n735: const G2_PROMPTS = new Map(G2_ROWS.map((row) => [row.id, row.prompt]));\n736: \n737: const SETS = Object.freeze({\n738:   chat: chatRowIds,\n739:   [G2_SET]: () => G2_ROWS.map((row) => row.id),\n740: });\n741: \n742: // `--list` and `--set` name the same thing. One prints the rows and the other\n743: // runs them, and an invocation that asks for both lists what it would run.\n744: function setNameOf(options) {\n745:   return options.list === null ? options.set : options.list;\n746: }\n747: \n748: function setRowIds(setName) {\n749:   if (!has(SETS, setName)) throw new UsageError(`unknown set: ${setName}`);\n750:   return SETS[setName]();\n751: }\n752: \n753: // A fixed prompt carries its own text, so its id needs no corpus row. Every other\n754: // id passes the lookup a run would use, so an id arriving on the command line and\n755: // nothing else is still checked before it can become an arm's row.\n756: function assertRunnable(rowId) {\n757:   if (!G2_PROMPTS.has(rowId)) findRow(rowId);\n758:   return rowId;\n759: }\n760: \n761: function selectedRowIds(options) {\n762:   const setName = setNameOf(options);\n763:   const fromSet = setName === null ? [] : setRowIds(setName);\n764:   const explicit = options.rowIds.map(assertRunnable);\n765:   return [...new Set([...fromSet, ...explicit])];\n766: }\n767: \n768: function fixtureDir(options) {\n769:   return options.fixture ?? resolve(dirname(options.out), G2_FIXTURE_DIRNAME);\n770: }\n771: \n772: // A placeholder keeps the fixed prompts free of a path that only exists on the\n773: // machine the arm runs on.\n774: function promptFor(rowId, fixture) {\n775:   const fixed = G2_PROMPTS.get(rowId);\n776:   if (fixed === undefined) return lookupPrompt(rowId).prompt;\n777:   return fixed.split(FIXTURE_TOKEN).join(fixture);\n778: }\n779: \n780: // The cap belongs to the prompt rather than to the invocation, so an arm that\n781: // mixes the two sets still gives a started workflow the time it needs.\n782: function capForRow(rowId) {\n783:   return G2_PROMPTS.has(rowId) ? CAP_MS.started : CAP_MS.chat;\n784: }\n785: \n786: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n787: // One turn\n788: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n789: \n790: // A turn runs with bypassed permissions, so it gets only what a runtime needs to\n791: // start and authenticate, and every other credential in the operator's shell stays\n792: // out. The shell basics and each runtime's own prefixes are enough for a CLI to\n793: // find its home, account and route, while a dispatched worker's marker is not\n794: // passed at all, because a CLI that answers as a child session is not the chat\n795: // start an arm measures.\n796: //\n797: // The network credentials stay out of the allowlist for the same reason: a turn\n798: // that reaches a forge with the operator's identity is a side effect no transcript\n799: // can undo. The GitHub CLI is pointed at an empty folder beside the run log rather\n800: // than the operator's authenticated one, and an empty credential helper replaces\n801: // whatever a global git config would have offered. A missing credential then fails\n802: // at once instead of waiting on a terminal nobody is sitting at.\n803: const CHILD_ENV_NAMES = Object.freeze([\n804:   'PATH',\n805:   'HOME',\n806:   'USER',\n807:   'LOGNAME',\n808:   'SHELL',\n809:   'TMPDIR',\n810:   'TMP',\n811:   'TEMP',\n812:   'LANG',\n813:   'TERM',\n814:   'TERM_PROGRAM',\n815:   'COLORTERM',\n816:   'NO_COLOR',\n817: ]);\n818: \n819: const CHILD_ENV_PREFIXES = Object.freeze([\n820:   'LC_',\n821:   'XDG_',\n822:   'ANTHROPIC_',\n823:   'CLAUDE_',\n824:   'CODEX_',\n825:   'OPENAI_',\n826:   'OPENCODE_',\n827:   'LLMGATEWAY_',\n828:   'PI_',\n829:   'DEVIN_',\n830:   'DSH_',\n831:   'DEEPSEEK_',\n832:   'OLLAMA_',\n833:   'SPECKIT_',\n834:   'SKILL_ADVISOR_',\n835:   'SYSTEM_SPEC_GATE_',\n836:   'MK_',\n837: ]);\n838: \n839: const GIT_CREDENTIAL_OVERRIDES = Object.freeze({\n840:   GIT_CONFIG_COUNT: '1',\n841:   GIT_CONFIG_KEY_0: 'credential.helper',\n842:   GIT_CONFIG_VALUE_0: '',\n843: });\n844: \n845: // The empty folder lives beside the run log, so a run leaves its own directory\n846: // behind instead of a path in the operator's home that the next run inherits.\n847: function ghConfigDir(outPath) {\n848:   return join(dirname(outPath), 'gh-config-empty');\n849: }\n850: \n851: export function childEnv(ghConfig) {\n852:   const env = {};\n853:   for (const [name, value] of Object.entries(process.env)) {\n854:     if (value === undefined) continue;\n855:     const keep =\n856:       CHILD_ENV_NAMES.includes(name) ||\n857:       CHILD_ENV_PREFIXES.some((prefix) => name.startsWith(prefix));\n858:     if (keep) env[name] = value;\n859:   }\n860:   env.GH_CONFIG_DIR = ghConfig;\n861:   env.GIT_TERMINAL_PROMPT = '0';\n862:   Object.assign(env, GIT_CREDENTIAL_OVERRIDES);\n863:   return env;\n864: }\n865: \n866: function spawnTurn(runtime, argv, prompt, capMs, ghConfig) {\n867:   return new Promise((settle) => {\n868:     // A CLI that reads the prompt from stdin gets a pipe to write it into. Every other\n869:     // runtime keeps stdin ignored, because an open stdin leaves that CLI waiting on\n870:     // input nobody types until the cap runs out into an empty transcript.\n871:     const child = spawn(runtime.command, argv, {\n872:       cwd: REPO_ROOT,\n873:       env: childEnv(ghConfig),\n874:       // Ignored means the child reads /dev/null rather than blocking on a terminal.\n875:       stdio: [runtime.promptOnStdin ? 'pipe' : 'ignore', 'pipe', 'inherit'],\n876:       // A chat turn still running after its cap is stuck, while a started workflow\n877:       // legitimately needs the longer one. The prompt decides which it gets.\n878:       timeout: capMs,\n879:     });\n880:     // The prompt is written and the stream closed at once, so a CLI that reads until\n881:     // the end of input sees a whole prompt. A CLI that exits before reading leaves a\n882:     // broken pipe behind, and that write error is the CLI's failure to report rather\n883:     // than an uncaught error that would end the run early.\n884:     if (child.stdin !== null) {\n885:       child.stdin.on('error', () => {});\n886:       child.stdin.end(prompt);\n887:     }\n888:     const locator = streamLocator(runtime.locator.field ?? null);\n889:     // The turn's own output is still shown, and the locator is read out of the\n890:     // same bytes, so watching a run and recording it never diverge.\n891:     child.stdout.setEncoding('utf8');\n892:     child.stdout.on('data', (chunk) => {\n893:       locator.push(chunk);\n894:       process.stdout.write(chunk);\n895:     });\n896:     child.on('error', (error) => settle({ exit: null, error, streamId: null }));\n897:     child.on('close', (exit) => settle({ exit, error: null, streamId: locator.value() }));\n898:   });\n899: }\n900: \n901: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n902: // CLI\n903: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n904: \n905: class UsageError extends Error {}\n906: \n907: const SCRIPT_NAME = 'run-command-behaviour.mjs';\n908: const SAMPLE_PROMPT = 'sample prompt for argv inspection';\n909: const SAMPLE_SESSION_ID = 'sample-session-id';\n910: \n911: // An arm label decides how a later scorer groups a whole log, so a typo would\n912: // quietly invent a third arm. The set is closed for that reason.\n913: const ARMS = ['pre', 'post', 'smoke', 'g2'];\n914: \n915: // The pre-change arm is the default because it is the one that can run before the\n916: // brief lands. A post-change run has to say which arm it belongs to. The\n917: // started-command set carries its own arm, so a run of it cannot land in the\n918: // change comparison by omission.\n919: const DEFAULT_ARM = 'pre';\n920: \n921: const USAGE = [\n922:   `usage: ${SCRIPT_NAME} --print-argv <runtime>`,\n923:   `       ${SCRIPT_NAME} --list <set>`,\n924:   `       ${SCRIPT_NAME} --runtime <runtime> (--set <set> | --row-id <id>...)`,\n925:   '         --out <run-log> [--arm <arm>] [--fixture <dir>] [--dry-run]',\n926:   `       ${SCRIPT_NAME} --backfill <run-log> --out <new-log> [--brief-only]`,\n927: ].join('\\n');\n928: \n929: function resolveRuntime(name) {\n930:   if (has(RUNTIMES, name)) {\n931:     return RUNTIMES[name];\n932:   }\n933:   if (has(NOT_MEASURED, name)) {\n934:     throw new UsageError(`runtime ${name} is not measured: ${NOT_MEASURED[name]}`);\n935:   }\n936:   throw new UsageError(`unknown runtime: ${name}`);\n937: }\n938: \n939: function valueAfter(argv, index, flag) {\n940:   const value = argv[index];\n941:   if (typeof value !== 'string' || value.startsWith('--')) {\n942:     throw new UsageError(`${flag}: a value is required`);\n943:   }\n944:   return value;\n945: }\n946: \n947: // A set that has its own arm names it, so a started-command run cannot join the\n948: // change comparison because nobody passed `--arm`.\n949: function armFor(options) {\n950:   if (options.arm !== null) return options.arm;\n951:   return options.set === G2_SET ? G2_SET : DEFAULT_ARM;\n952: }\n953: \n954: function parseOptions(argv) {\n955:   const options = {\n956:     printArgv: null,\n957:     list: null,\n958:     set: null,\n959:     runtime: null,\n960:     rowIds: [],\n961:     arm: null,\n962:     out: null,\n963:     backfill: null,\n964:     fixture: null,\n965:     dryRun: false,\n966:     briefOnly: false,\n967:   };\n968:   for (let index = 0; index < argv.length; index += 1) {\n969:     const token = argv[index];\n970:     if (token === '--print-argv') {\n971:       index += 1;\n972:       options.printArgv = valueAfter(argv, index, '--print-argv');\n973:     } else if (token === '--list') {\n974:       index += 1;\n975:       options.list = valueAfter(argv, index, '--list');\n976:     } else if (token === '--set') {\n977:       index += 1;\n978:       options.set = valueAfter(argv, index, '--set');\n979:     } else if (token === '--runtime') {\n980:       index += 1;\n981:       options.runtime = valueAfter(argv, index, '--runtime');\n982:     } else if (token === '--row-id') {\n983:       index += 1;\n984:       options.rowIds.push(valueAfter(argv, index, '--row-id'));\n985:     } else if (token === '--arm') {\n986:       index += 1;\n987:       const arm = valueAfter(argv, index, '--arm');\n988:       if (!ARMS.includes(arm)) {\n989:         throw new UsageError(`--arm: expected one of ${ARMS.join(', ')}`);\n990:       }\n991:       options.arm = arm;\n992:     } else if (token === '--out') {\n993:       index += 1;\n994:       options.out = resolve(valueAfter(argv, index, '--out'));\n995:     } else if (token === '--backfill') {\n996:       index += 1;\n997:       options.backfill = resolve(valueAfter(argv, index, '--backfill'));\n998:     } else if (token === '--fixture') {\n999:       index += 1;\n1000:       options.fixture = resolve(valueAfter(argv, index, '--fixture'));\n1001:     } else if (token === '--dry-run') {\n1002:       options.dryRun = true;\n1003:     } else if (token === '--brief-only') {\n1004:       options.briefOnly = true;\n1005:     } else {\n1006:       throw new UsageError(`unknown option: ${token}`);\n1007:     }\n1008:   }\n1009:   return options;\n1010: }\n1011: \n1012: // One mode per invocation. Asking for an argv and a run at once would hide which\n1013: // one the operator meant, and a run with no log has nowhere to record its proof.\n1014: function selectMode(options) {\n1015:   // Only a backfill rewrites delivery, so the flag on any other invocation would\n1016:   // parse and then quietly change nothing.\n1017:   if (options.briefOnly && options.backfill === null) {\n1018:     throw new UsageError('--brief-only needs --backfill');\n1019:   }\n1020:   if (options.printArgv !== null) return 'print-argv';\n1021:   if (options.list !== null) return 'list';\n1022:   if (options.backfill !== null) {\n1023:     if (options.out === null) throw new UsageError('--out is required');\n1024:     return 'backfill';\n1025:   }\n1026:   if (options.runtime === null) throw new UsageError('--runtime is required');\n1027:   if (options.set === null && options.rowIds.length === 0) {\n1028:     throw new UsageError('--set or --row-id is required');\n1029:   }\n1030:   if (options.out === null) throw new UsageError('--out is required');\n1031:   return 'run';\n1032: }\n1033: \n1034: function writeJson(value) {\n1035:   process.stdout.write(`${JSON.stringify(value)}\\n`);\n1036: }\n1037: \n1038: function writeRowIds(rowIds) {\n1039:   process.stdout.write(rowIds.map((rowId) => `${rowId}\\n`).join(''));\n1040: }\n1041: \n1042: function writeArgv(runtimeName) {\n1043:   const runtime = resolveRuntime(runtimeName);\n1044:   const argv = runtime.buildArgv({ prompt: SAMPLE_PROMPT, sessionId: SAMPLE_SESSION_ID });\n1045:   // The prompt rides stdin, so it must not leak into the argv an operator inspects.\n1046:   if (runtime.promptOnStdin && argv.includes(SAMPLE_PROMPT)) {\n1047:     throw new Error(`${runtimeName}: prompt is on stdin but also in argv`);\n1048:   }\n1049:   writeJson(argv);\n1050: }\n1051: \n1052: // One prompt and its clean-up. The returned status ends the arm as soon as a guard\n1053: // fires, so a broken precondition never spends another model call.\n1054: async function runTurn(job, options, exempt, isFirst) {\n1055:   const before = { status: statusPaths(), ...gitSnapshot() };\n1056:   if (isFirst) {\n1057:     // A live turn can push, so it may only start in the arena the builder made: no\n1058:     // remote to push to and the builder's marker in place. A dry run spawns nothing\n1059:     // and keeps running in this checkout.\n1060:     if (!options.dryRun) {\n1061:       const refusal = liveArmRefusal(REPO_ROOT);\n1062:       if (refusal !== null) {\n1063:         return { status: EXIT_REFUSED, refusal };\n1064:       }\n1065:     }\n1066:     const dirty = dirtyPaths(before.status, exempt);\n1067:     if (dirty.length > 0) {\n1068:       return {\n1069:         status: EXIT_REFUSED,\n1070:         refusal: { ok: false, code: 'worktree_dirty', paths: dirty },\n1071:       };\n1072:     }\n1073:   }\n1074: \n1075:   const sessionId = randomUUID();\n1076:   const argv = job.runtime.buildArgv({ prompt: job.prompt, sessionId });\n1077:   // A runtime that names its session only in its own store is bracketed by a\n1078:   // listing, and the entry the turn added is the session it wrote. A dry run\n1079:   // starts no CLI, so it has nothing to list.\n1080:   const listed = !options.dryRun && listsStore(job.runtime);\n1081:   const listingBefore = listed ? storeIds(job.runtime, job.ghConfig) : null;\n1082:   const turn = options.dryRun\n1083:     ? { exit: null, error: null, streamId: null }\n1084:     : await spawnTurn(job.runtime, argv, job.prompt, job.cap, job.ghConfig);\n1085:   const reportedId = listed\n1086:     ? addedSessionId(listingBefore, storeIds(job.runtime, job.ghConfig))\n1087:     : turn.streamId;\n1088: \n1089:   const after = { status: statusPaths(), ...gitSnapshot() };\n1090:   const headMoved = before.head !== after.head || before.stash !== after.stash;\n1091:   const changed = changedPaths(before.status, after.status);\n1092:   // The locator is kept in hand because the brief reader opens the same session\n1093:   // the line records, and resolving it twice could name a different file.\n1094:   const locator = locatorFor(job.runtime, sessionId, reportedId);\n1095:   const line = {\n1096:     runtime: job.runtimeName,\n1097:     rowId: job.rowId,\n1098:     arm: options.arm,\n1099:     locator,\n1100:     exit: turn.exit,\n1101:     changedPaths: changed,\n1102:   };\n1103: \n1104:   if (headMoved) {\n1105:     // The worktree the restore was written for is gone. Restoring now could discard\n1106:     // whatever the commit or stash brought in, so the paths stay listed instead.\n1107:     appendRunLine(options.out, { ...line, restored: false, headMoved: true });\n1108:     return { status: EXIT_HEAD_MOVED, refusal: { ok: false, code: 'head_moved', rowId: job.rowId } };\n1109:   }\n1110: \n1111:   // A dry run spawns nothing, so an unchanged worktree is the only thing it can\n1112:   // honestly report as left as found.\n1113:   const restore = options.dryRun ? null : restoreTurn({ cwd: REPO_ROOT, before: before.status });\n1114:   const restored = options.dryRun ? changed.length === 0 : restore.restored;\n1115:   // The head the reader saw is the only place the offer was named, and not every\n1116:   // runtime keeps it, so the offer is replayed after the turn. A dry run still\n1117:   // replays it, which lets an operator read the offer without paying for a model\n1118:   // call, while the brief stays `unknown` because no session exists to read.\n1119:   const offer = replayOffer({ checkoutDir: PROJECT_DIR, prompt: job.prompt, sessionId });\n1120:   // Pi, Devin and DSH keep the brief in their session store rather than in the\n1121:   // turn records, so the delivery check needs the reader that opens that store.\n1122:   const delivered = options.dryRun\n1123:     ? 'unknown'\n1124:     : await briefDelivered({ runtime: job.runtimeName, locator, loadSession: loadSharedSession });\n1125:   appendRunLine(options.out, {\n1126:     ...line,\n1127:     offeredCommand: offer.offeredCommand,\n1128:     offeredPolicy: offer.offeredPolicy,\n1129:     briefDelivered: delivered,\n1130:     restored,\n1131:     ...(restore !== null && restore.leftoverPaths.length > 0\n1132:       ? { leftoverPaths: restore.leftoverPaths }\n1133:       : {}),\n1134:   });\n1135: \n1136:   if (turn.error !== null) {\n1137:     const detail = turn.error.message;\n1138:     return { status: 1, refusal: { ok: false, code: 'spawn_failed', runtime: job.runtimeName, detail } };\n1139:   }\n1140:   if (!restored) {\n1141:     return {\n1142:       status: 1,\n1143:       refusal: {\n1144:         ok: false,\n1145:         code: 'restore_failed',\n1146:         rowId: job.rowId,\n1147:         paths: changed,\n1148:         ...(restore === null ? {} : { leftoverPaths: restore.leftoverPaths }),\n1149:       },\n1150:     };\n1151:   }\n1152:   return { status: 0, refusal: null };\n1153: }\n1154: \n1155: async function runArm(options, rowIds) {\n1156:   const exempt = exemptPrefix(dirname(options.out));\n1157:   const runtime = resolveRuntime(options.runtime);\n1158:   const fixture = fixtureDir(options);\n1159:   // Created once before the first snapshot, so the folder a turn needs is never\n1160:   // read back as a path the turn itself changed.\n1161:   const ghConfig = ghConfigDir(options.out);\n1162:   mkdirSync(ghConfig, { recursive: true });\n1163:   for (let index = 0; index < rowIds.length; index += 1) {\n1164:     const rowId = rowIds[index];\n1165:     const prompt = promptFor(rowId, fixture);\n1166:     const job = { runtime, runtimeName: options.runtime, rowId, prompt, cap: capForRow(rowId), ghConfig };\n1167:     const result = await runTurn(job, options, exempt, index === 0);\n1168:     if (result.refusal !== null) writeJson(result.refusal);\n1169:     if (result.status !== 0) return result.status;\n1170:   }\n1171:   return 0;\n1172: }\n1173: \n1174: // A finished log is rewritten from its recorded lines alone, and the fixture folder\n1175: // still sits beside the log the arm wrote. The corpus lookup runs before the first\n1176: // line is written, so a row the dataset guard refuses leaves no output file behind.\n1177: async function backfillLog(options) {\n1178:   const fixture = resolve(dirname(options.backfill), G2_FIXTURE_DIRNAME);\n1179:   const result = await backfillRunLog({\n1180:     inPath: options.backfill,\n1181:     outPath: options.out,\n1182:     promptFor: (rowId) => promptFor(rowId, fixture),\n1183:     briefOnly: options.briefOnly,\n1184:   });\n1185:   writeJson(result);\n1186:   return result.ok ? 0 : EXIT_REFUSED;\n1187: }\n1188: \n1189: async function main(argv) {\n1190:   try {\n1191:     const options = parseOptions(argv);\n1192:     // Selection runs before the mode check, so an id the dataset guard refuses is\n1193:     // answered by that guard rather than by whichever usage check would run first.\n1194:     const rowIds = selectedRowIds(options);\n1195:     const mode = selectMode(options);\n1196:     options.arm = armFor(options);\n1197: \n1198:     if (mode === 'print-argv') {\n1199:       writeArgv(options.printArgv);\n1200:       return 0;\n1201:     }\n1202:     if (mode === 'list') {\n1203:       writeRowIds(rowIds);\n1204:       return 0;\n1205:     }\n1206:     if (mode === 'backfill') {\n1207:       return await backfillLog(options);\n1208:     }\n1209:     // An unapproved project hook makes a Codex arm produce zeros that read like\n1210:     // refusals, so that arm is not measurable. No turn decides this answer, so a\n1211:     // dry run is refused here too and nothing writes the approval.\n1212:     if (\n1213:       options.runtime === 'codex'\n1214:       && codexHookApproval({ projectDir: PROJECT_DIR }) === 'unapproved'\n1215:     ) {\n1216:       writeJson({\n1217:         ok: false,\n1218:         code: 'codex-hooks-unapproved',\n1219:         runtime: 'codex',\n1220:         measurable: false,\n1221:         repair: `open /hooks in a Codex session at ${PROJECT_DIR} and approve each entry`,\n1222:       });\n1223:       return EXIT_REFUSED;\n1224:     }\n1225:     return await runArm(options, rowIds);\n1226:   } catch (error) {\n1227:     if (error instanceof UsageError) {\n1228:       process.stderr.write(`${SCRIPT_NAME}: ${error.message}\\n${USAGE}\\n`);\n1229:       return EXIT_REFUSED;\n1230:     }\n1231:     if (error instanceof Refusal) {\n1232:       writeJson({ ok: false, code: error.code, detail: error.message });\n1233:       return EXIT_REFUSED;\n1234:     }\n1235:     throw error;\n1236:   }\n1237: }\n1238: \n1239: // The runner's tables are exported for direct tests, so the CLI runs only when\n1240: // this file is the entry point. An imported module that parsed the host's argv\n1241: // would refuse on flags it never received. The entry path is compared after\n1242: // resolving links, because Node names a linked entry by its real path.\n1243: const invokedDirectly = (() => {\n[Output truncated. Continue reading with offset: 1244]","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":true},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/run-command-behaviour.mjs, lines 1-1243\n1: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n2: // MODULE: One-Turn Command Behaviour Runner\n3: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n4: //\n5: // Command surfacing is only proven by a turn that starts the way an operator\n6: // starts it: one prompt, the real CLI, and a session id the transcript reader\n7: // can open afterwards. Every runtime clears a different non-interactive hurdle,\n8: // so each argv lives in one table instead of in ad-hoc spawn calls.\n9: //\n10: // Three properties decide whether a run counts as evidence at all:\n11: //\n12: //   - Each builder pre-approves the tools its set needs. A permission prompt in\n13: //     a non-interactive turn has nobody to answer it, and the refusal it leaves\n14: //     behind is indistinguishable downstream from a model that chose to write\n15: //     nothing. Without this, every must-not-start row passes for the wrong\n16: //     reason.\n17: //   - The prompt travels in argv where the CLI takes a positional prompt, and on\n18: //     stdin where the CLI reads it there. Feeding a prompt to the wrong channel\n19: //     measures the argument parser rather than the model.\n20: //   - A turn is capped by the prompt it runs. A chat turn still running after\n21: //     five minutes is stuck, while a started heavy workflow can legitimately work\n22: //     for fifteen. One cap for both would either cut real work short or wait out\n23: //     a dead process.\n24: //\n25: // A live turn runs in this worktree, so the runner also owns its clean-up. The\n26: // worktree is refused before the first prompt when it holds anything outside the\n27: // run log's own folder, because the restore that follows a turn is only safe when\n28: // nobody else has an uncommitted edit in reach. Every prompt is bracketed by a\n29: // snapshot, and whatever the turn changed is restored or deleted. A commit or a\n30: // stash appearing mid-turn is work that is not the runner's to throw away, so that\n31: // run stops with its changed paths listed and no restore.\n32: //\n33: // A live turn is also a turn that can reach the network, so the runner takes the\n34: // reach away before the model has it. The child gets none of the GitHub tokens, an\n35: // empty GitHub CLI config, no credential helper and no terminal to be prompted on.\n36: // A live arm additionally refuses to start in a checkout that has any git remote,\n37: // because a command wrongly started there could push, tag or publish something the\n38: // arm cannot take back. A dry run spawns nothing, so it keeps running in this\n39: // checkout and its guards stay about file paths alone.\n40: //\n41: // Usage:\n42: //   node run-command-behaviour.mjs --print-argv <runtime>\n43: //   node run-command-behaviour.mjs --list <set>\n44: //   node run-command-behaviour.mjs --runtime <runtime> (--set <set> | --row-id <id>...)\n45: //     --out <run-log> [--arm <arm>] [--fixture <dir>] [--dry-run]\n46: //   node run-command-behaviour.mjs --backfill <run-log> --out <new-log> [--brief-only]\n47: \n48: import { appendFileSync, existsSync, mkdirSync, readdirSync, readFileSync, realpathSync } from 'node:fs';\n49: import { dirname, join, resolve } from 'node:path';\n50: import { homedir } from 'node:os';\n51: import { randomUUID } from 'node:crypto';\n52: import { spawn, spawnSync } from 'node:child_process';\n53: import { fileURLToPath } from 'node:url';\n54: import { dshProjectKey, dshStoreRoot } from '../../../../../hooks/shared/session-readers/dsh.mjs';\n55: import { loadSession as loadSharedSession } from '../../../../../hooks/shared/session-readers/index.mjs';\n56: import { backfillRunLog, briefDelivered, codexHookApproval, replayOffer } from './command-offer.mjs';\n57: import { restoreTurn, statusSnapshot } from './worktree-restore.mjs';\n58: \n59: const HERE = dirname(fileURLToPath(import.meta.url));\n60: const REPO_ROOT = resolve(HERE, '../../../../../..');\n61: const DSH_PATCH_DIR = resolve(REPO_ROOT, '.dsh/patches');\n62: // A stored session is found by its id plus the directory the turn ran in, and the\n63: // scorer reads it back from another checkout, so the locator records the resolved\n64: // directory instead of letting each reader compare the id against its own root.\n65: const PROJECT_DIR = realpathSync(REPO_ROOT);\n66: const PROMPTS_PATH = resolve(HERE, 'command-prompts.jsonl');\n67: \n68: const EXIT_REFUSED = 2;\n69: const EXIT_HEAD_MOVED = 3;\n70: \n71: // Keyed by prompt kind rather than by set name, because one arm can hold rows\n72: // from either set and the cap has to follow the row, not the invocation.\n73: const CAP_MS = Object.freeze({\n74:   chat: 300_000,\n75:   started: 900_000,\n76: });\n77: \n78: const CLAUDE_MODEL = 'claude-sonnet-5-5';\n79: const CODEX_MODEL = 'gpt-6-luna';\n80: // The variant rides the model string, because `opencode run` has no flag for it.\n81: const OPENCODE_MODEL = 'llmgateway/deepseek-v4.1-flash-1017#max';\n82: const PI_MODEL = 'llmgateway/deepseek-v4.1-flash-1017';\n83: const DEVIN_MODEL = 'swe-2-max';\n84: const DSH_MODEL = 'deepseek-v4.1-flash-1017';\n85: \n86: // Runtimes the phase reports as not measured, with the reason a run would be\n87: // wasted work rather than evidence. Cursor's reader never leaves gated, so its\n88: // transcript could not be scored even if the turn ran.\n89: const NOT_MEASURED = Object.freeze({\n90:   cursor: 'its session reader is gated, so no produced transcript can be scored',\n91: });\n92: \n93: function has(object, key) {\n94:   return Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(object, key);\n95: }\n96: \n97: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n98: // Runtime argv table\n99: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n100: \n101: const RUNTIMES = {\n102:   claude: {\n103:     command: 'claude',\n104:     model: CLAUDE_MODEL,\n105:     promptOnStdin: false,\n106:     // stream-json writes one JSON object per line and every one of them carries\n107:     // the session id the transcript is stored under.\n108:     locator: { kind: 'stream', field: 'session_id' },\n109:     // The reader opens a transcript by id plus its own path, and Claude Code names\n110:     // the file after the session inside a folder per working directory.\n111:     findTranscript: claudeTranscriptPath,\n112:     buildArgv: ({ prompt }) => [\n113:       '-p',\n114:       prompt,\n115:       '--model',\n116:       CLAUDE_MODEL,\n117:       // stream-json is the only output format that carries the session id the\n118:       // reader needs after the turn ends.\n119:       '--output-format',\n120:       'stream-json',\n121:       '--verbose',\n122:       // Print mode has nobody to answer an approval prompt, and the default mode\n123:       // both denies writes and refuses shell commands, which is exactly the\n124:       // behaviour an ask-row test must be free to observe.\n125:       '--permission-mode',\n126:       'bypassPermissions',\n127:     ],\n128:   },\n129:   codex: {\n130:     command: 'codex',\n131:     model: CODEX_MODEL,\n132:     promptOnStdin: true,\n133:     // The run opens with an event naming its thread, and the rollout file is\n134:     // named after that thread id.\n135:     locator: { kind: 'stream', field: 'thread_id' },\n136:     // The rollout file is named after the thread id the stream reported, so the id\n137:     // alone leads back to it once the store has been searched.\n138:     findTranscript: codexTranscriptPath,\n139:     buildArgv: () => [\n140:       'exec',\n141:       // The JSON event stream is how a turn reports its thread id.\n142:       '--json',\n143:       '--model',\n144:       CODEX_MODEL,\n145:       '-c',\n146:       'model_reasoning_effort=medium',\n147:       '-c',\n148:       'service_tier=fast',\n149:       // A headless run cannot answer an approval prompt, and `exec` defaults to a\n150:       // read-only sandbox that would silently swallow every write.\n151:       '-c',\n152:       'approval_policy=never',\n153:       '--sandbox',\n154:       'workspace-write',\n155:       '-',\n156:     ],\n157:   },\n158:   opencode: {\n159:     command: 'opencode',\n160:     model: OPENCODE_MODEL,\n161:     promptOnStdin: false,\n162:     // The turn's own events carry the id of the session it created.\n163:     locator: { kind: 'stream', field: 'sessionID' },\n164:     buildArgv: ({ prompt }) => [\n165:       // `opencode run` takes no --dir, and the child already starts in the\n166:       // checkout, so passing the directory a second time would only fail.\n167:       'run',\n168:       // Without this the turn is handed to the operator's long-running background\n169:       // service, whose tools run with that service's environment. The hardened\n170:       // environment this runner builds would then never reach a tool call.\n171:       '--standalone',\n172:       '--model',\n173:       OPENCODE_MODEL,\n174:       '--format',\n175:       'json',\n176:       // Auto-approve everything not explicitly denied. A run that stalls on a\n177:       // permission prompt reads as a model that declined to act.\n178:       '--auto',\n179:       prompt,\n180:     ],\n181:   },\n182:   pi: {\n183:     command: 'pi',\n184:     model: PI_MODEL,\n185:     promptOnStdin: false,\n186:     // Print mode never reports the session it wrote, so the runner mints the id\n187:     // and passes it on the command line instead of recovering it from the stream.\n188:     locator: { kind: 'runner' },\n189:     buildArgv: ({ prompt, sessionId }) => [\n190:       '-p',\n191:       // Without it a non-interactive Pi probes the network on startup and can\n192:       // hang before it ever reads the prompt.\n193:       '--offline',\n194:       '--model',\n195:       PI_MODEL,\n196:       // Without a minted id the log would have to be guessed from whatever\n197:       // file is newest, which can open a neighbouring turn's transcript.\n198:       '--session-id',\n199:       sessionId,\n200:       prompt,\n201:     ],\n202:   },\n203:   devin: {\n204:     command: 'devin',\n205:     model: DEVIN_MODEL,\n206:     promptOnStdin: false,\n207:     // A Devin turn keeps its session in a store its own output does not name, so\n208:     // the id comes from the listing of the directory the turn ran in.\n209:     locator: { kind: 'listing', args: ['list', '--format', 'json'] },\n210:     buildArgv: ({ prompt }) => [\n211:       '-p',\n212:       '--model',\n213:       DEVIN_MODEL,\n214:       // Under `accept-edits` every tool call that wants confirmation is refused,\n215:       // so a headless turn explores its whole budget and writes nothing.\n216:       '--permission-mode',\n217:       'dangerous',\n218:       // A fresh checkout has never been trusted interactively, and print mode has\n219:       // nobody to answer the trust prompt, so the turn would refuse to start.\n220:       '--respect-workspace-trust',\n221:       'false',\n222:       // No `--sandbox` here. That flag forces a permission mode that ignores\n223:       // `dangerous` and rejects every write in print mode, so a turn would write\n224:       // nothing and read as a model that declined. Containment comes from the\n225:       // arena instead.\n226:       // The separator keeps a prompt that opens with a dash from parsing as a flag.\n227:       '--',\n228:       prompt,\n229:     ],\n230:   },\n231:   dsh: {\n232:     command: 'dsh',\n233:     // dsh takes no model flag at all. The roster route patch decides the model,\n234:     // and this table pins the verified LLM Gateway route.\n235:     model: DSH_MODEL,\n236:     promptOnStdin: true,\n237:     // The harness names the session itself and refuses an id it never created,\n238:     // so the turn's id is recovered from the store folder its log lands in.\n239:     locator: { kind: 'store', list: dshSessionIds },\n240:     buildArgv: () => [\n241:       '--profile',\n242:       'headless',\n243:       '--patch',\n244:       resolve(DSH_PATCH_DIR, 'route-llmgateway-deepseek.patch.yml'),\n245:       // No lineage patch: it marks the run as a fan-out leaf, and the repo\n246:       // guards keep the prompt advisor off for a leaf. A chat turn has to\n247:       // receive the brief like a person's session would.\n248:       '--patch',\n249:       resolve(DSH_PATCH_DIR, 'guards.patch.yml'),\n250:       '--json',\n251:       '--permission-mode',\n252:       'workspace-write',\n253:       // Approvals default to ask, and a headless turn has nobody to answer.\n254:       '--approval-policy',\n255:       'never',\n256:       // The dash takes the prompt from stdin, which is the channel a headless\n257:       // run reads, and keeps a prompt that opens with one from parsing as a\n258:       // flag.\n259:       '-',\n260:     ],\n261:   },\n262: };\n263: \n264: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n265: // Session locator\n266: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n267: //\n268: // A run log is only evidence if it leads back to the exact transcript the turn\n269: // wrote, and each runtime exposes that handle in its own way. Three sources\n270: // exist: an id the CLI reports after the turn, either in its output or in the\n271: // session listing it keeps, an id the runner hands the CLI before it starts, or\n272: // no recorded source at all, which the log states rather than filling with the\n273: // newest file in the store.\n274: \n275: // Output arrives in chunks that cut a line in half, so a line is parsed only\n276: // once its newline has come through. Parsing stops at the first match, because\n277: // the id never changes within one turn.\n278: function streamLocator(field) {\n279:   let pending = '';\n280:   let captured = null;\n281:   return {\n282:     push(chunk) {\n283:       if (field === null || captured !== null) return;\n284:       pending += chunk;\n285:       const lines = pending.split('\\n');\n286:       pending = lines.pop() ?? '';\n287:       for (const line of lines) {\n288:         captured = readId(line, field);\n289:         if (captured !== null) return;\n290:       }\n291:     },\n292:     value() {\n293:       if (captured === null && pending !== '') captured = readId(pending, field);\n294:       return captured;\n295:     },\n296:   };\n297: }\n298: \n299: // A line that is not a JSON object is ordinary chatter, so a parse failure says\n300: // this line holds no locator rather than that the run failed.\n301: function readId(line, field) {\n302:   let event;\n303:   try {\n304:     event = JSON.parse(line);\n305:   } catch {\n306:     return null;\n307:   }\n308:   const id = event?.[field];\n309:   return typeof id === 'string' && id !== '' ? id : null;\n310: }\n311: \n312: // A runtime that keeps its sessions in a store its output never names still lists\n313: // them for the directory the turn ran in, so two listings taken around a turn\n314: // reduce the store to a set difference. The listing runs in the child's\n315: // environment, because that is the store the turn itself wrote to.\n316: const LISTING_CAP_MS = 60_000;\n317: \n318: function listingIds(runtime, ghConfig) {\n319:   const source = runtime.locator;\n320:   if (source.kind !== 'listing') return null;\n321:   const result = spawnSync(runtime.command, source.args, {\n322:     cwd: REPO_ROOT,\n323:     env: childEnv(ghConfig),\n324:     encoding: 'utf8',\n325:     timeout: LISTING_CAP_MS,\n326:   });\n327:   if (result.error) return null;\n328:   return result.status === 0 ? listedSessionIds(result.stdout) : null;\n329: }\n330: \n331: // A runtime that names its session only in a store it owns is bracketed by two\n332: // readings of that store, and the entry the turn added is the session it wrote.\n333: function listsStore(runtime) {\n334:   return runtime.locator.kind === 'listing' || runtime.locator.kind === 'store';\n335: }\n336: \n337: // Only the reading differs between the store-backed forms: one CLI answers with\n338: // its own index and the other keeps one folder per session. Any other kind\n339: // names its session in the turn's output instead.\n340: function storeIds(runtime, ghConfig) {\n341:   return runtime.locator.kind === 'store'\n342:     ? runtime.locator.list()\n343:     : listingIds(runtime, ghConfig);\n344: }\n345: \n346: // The listing is a JSON array of session objects whose `id` field is the value\n347: // the CLI's own resume takes, so that field is the one that leads back to a\n348: // transcript. Any other shape names no session, which the caller reads as no id\n349: // rather than as a failed run.\n350: function listedSessionIds(text) {\n351:   let entries;\n352:   try {\n353:     entries = JSON.parse(text);\n354:   } catch {\n355:     return null;\n356:   }\n357:   if (!Array.isArray(entries)) return null;\n358:   return entries\n359:     .map((entry) => (entry !== null && typeof entry === 'object' ? entry.id : null))\n360:     .filter((id) => typeof id === 'string' && id !== '');\n361: }\n362: \n363: // A session another operator opened while the turn ran is not this turn's, and a\n364: // listing that reports none or several added entries has no id it can name with\n365: // confidence, so only a single added entry becomes the locator.\n366: function addedSessionId(before, after) {\n367:   if (before === null || after === null) return null;\n368:   const known = new Set(before);\n369:   const added = [...new Set(after.filter((id) => !known.has(id)))];\n370:   return added.length === 1 ? added[0] : null;\n371: }\n372: \n373: // DSH keeps its session logs under DSH_HOME, which an operator can move, so the\n374: // locator records the home the run used and the transcript stays findable from\n375: // the log alone.\n376: function dshHome() {\n377:   return process.env.DSH_HOME || join(homedir(), '.dsh');\n378: }\n379: \n380: // A DSH session is a folder named after its id under one folder per project the\n381: // harness keyed from the directory the turn ran in, and the turn's own output\n382: // never names that id. The store root comes from the reader so the folder listed\n383: // here is the folder the reader will search. A store the harness has not created\n384: // yet lists nothing, which is the same answer as a turn that wrote no session.\n385: function dshSessionIds() {\n386:   const store = join(dshStoreRoot(), dshProjectKey(PROJECT_DIR));\n387:   try {\n388:     return readdirSync(store, { withFileTypes: true })\n389:       .filter((entry) => entry.isDirectory())\n390:       .map((entry) => entry.name);\n391:   } catch {\n392:     return [];\n393:   }\n394: }\n395: \n396: // A store folder is the CLI's to lay out, so the walk follows the folders it\n397: // creates and matches on the file name instead of predicting a path. A store that\n398: // is missing or unreadable names no transcript, which is the same answer as a turn\n399: // that wrote none rather than a reason to fail the run.\n400: function findTranscriptFiles(dir, matches, depth) {\n401:   let entries;\n402:   try {\n403:     entries = readdirSync(dir, { withFileTypes: true });\n404:   } catch {\n405:     return [];\n406:   }\n407:   const found = [];\n408:   for (const entry of entries) {\n409:     if (entry.isFile() && matches(entry.name)) {\n410:       found.push(join(dir, entry.name));\n411:     } else if (entry.isDirectory() && depth > 0) {\n412:       found.push(...findTranscriptFiles(join(dir, entry.name), matches, depth - 1));\n413:     }\n414:   }\n415:   return found;\n416: }\n417: \n418: // One match is the turn's own transcript, while two leave the locator without a\n419: // path because choosing between them could score a neighbouring turn under this\n420: // row. The path is resolved so a log written from another checkout still opens.\n421: function storedTranscriptPath(root, matches, depth = Infinity) {\n422:   const found = findTranscriptFiles(root, matches, depth);\n423:   return found.length === 1 ? resolve(found[0]) : null;\n424: }\n425: \n426: // Claude Code keeps one folder per working directory and writes each transcript\n427: // directly inside it, so one level below the store is the whole of the search. A\n428: // helper transcript sits deeper inside `subagents/`, which belongs to another turn\n429: // and which the reader refuses.\n430: function claudeTranscriptPath(sessionId) {\n431:   const store = join(process.env.CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR || join(homedir(), '.claude'), 'projects');\n432:   return storedTranscriptPath(store, (name) => name === `${sessionId}.jsonl`, 1);\n433: }\n434: \n435: // A Codex rollout file is named after its start time and ends with the thread id,\n436: // and it sits under a date folder whose depth is the CLI's to choose, so the whole\n437: // store is searched and the id is a suffix of the name.\n438: function codexTranscriptPath(sessionId) {\n439:   const store = join(process.env.CODEX_HOME || join(homedir(), '.codex'), 'sessions');\n440:   return storedTranscriptPath(store, (name) => name.endsWith(`-${sessionId}.jsonl`));\n441: }\n442: \n443: // A store listing that added no single entry leaves the id out rather than\n444: // naming a guess that would score a neighbouring turn under this row. The home\n445: // stays on the record so the store the run wrote to is findable from the log\n446: // alone.\n447: function storeLocator(reportedId) {\n448:   const store = { dshHome: dshHome(), projectDir: PROJECT_DIR };\n449:   return reportedId === null ? store : { sessionId: reportedId, ...store };\n450: }\n451: \n452: function locatorFor(runtime, sessionId, reportedId) {\n453:   const source = runtime.locator;\n454:   // The id the runner handed the CLI is the one the store keys its log by, and\n455:   // the directory the turn ran in is what a reader matches the log's header\n456:   // against, so both travel with every record.\n457:   if (source.kind === 'runner') return { sessionId, projectDir: PROJECT_DIR };\n458:   if (source.kind === 'store') return storeLocator(reportedId);\n459:   // A runtime that reported no id records none, which keeps a scorer from opening\n460:   // a neighbouring transcript that happens to be newer.\n461:   if (reportedId === null) return null;\n462:   const locator = { sessionId: reportedId, projectDir: PROJECT_DIR };\n463:   // A reader needs the file the turn wrote as well as its id, so a store-based\n464:   // runtime resolves the id to a path once the turn has closed. A turn that left\n465:   // no single match keeps the field out rather than naming a guess.\n466:   const transcriptPath = typeof runtime.findTranscript === 'function'\n467:     ? runtime.findTranscript(reportedId)\n468:     : null;\n469:   if (transcriptPath !== null) locator.transcriptPath = transcriptPath;\n470:   return locator;\n471: }\n472: \n473: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n474: // Worktree guards\n475: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n476: //\n477: // Every live turn runs in this worktree, so the runner cleans up after itself and\n478: // refuses to start when the worktree already holds work that is not its own. The\n479: // order matters: the remote check runs before the dirty check, so a live refusal\n480: // names the one condition an arm can never repair, and the dirty check runs before\n481: // the first prompt, so a restore can never discard an edit another worker has not\n482: // committed yet, and the head check runs around every prompt, so a commit or a\n483: // stash taken mid-turn stops the arm before the next model call rather than after\n484: // the damage.\n485: \n486: // A filesystem monitor that decides a path is unchanged makes a status snapshot\n487: // lie, and the restore step trusts that snapshot.\n488: const GIT_PREFIX = ['-c', 'core.fsmonitor=false'];\n489: \n490: // The arena builder writes this file into the checkout it made. Its absence is the\n491: // proof that a root is an operator's working tree rather than a disposable arena.\n492: const MARK = 'skilled-behaviour-arena';\n493: \n494: function git(args) {\n495:   const result = spawnSync('git', [...GIT_PREFIX, ...args], {\n496:     cwd: REPO_ROOT,\n497:     encoding: 'utf8',\n498:   });\n499:   if (result.error) throw result.error;\n500:   return result;\n501: }\n502: \n503: function gitText(args) {\n504:   const result = git(args);\n505:   if (result.status !== 0) {\n506:     throw new Error(`git ${args.join(' ')} failed: ${(result.stderr ?? '').trim()}`);\n507:   }\n508:   return result.stdout;\n509: }\n510: \n511: function statusPaths() {\n512:   return statusSnapshot(REPO_ROOT);\n513: }\n514: \n515: // The out folder is this arm's own output area. Git is asked for that folder's path\n516: // from the top of the repository, because comparing two spellings of the same\n517: // folder misreads a root that is reached through a symlink. An out folder outside\n518: // any repository exempts nothing, which only makes the guard stricter.\n519: function exemptPrefix(outDir) {\n520:   mkdirSync(outDir, { recursive: true });\n521:   const result = spawnSync('git', [...GIT_PREFIX, 'rev-parse', '--show-prefix'], {\n522:     cwd: outDir,\n523:     encoding: 'utf8',\n524:   });\n525:   if (result.status !== 0) return null;\n526:   const prefix = result.stdout.trim();\n527:   if (prefix === '') return null;\n528:   return prefix.endsWith('/') ? prefix.slice(0, -1) : prefix;\n529: }\n530: \n531: function isUnderExempt(path, prefix) {\n532:   if (prefix === null) return false;\n533:   const trimmed = path.endsWith('/') ? path.slice(0, -1) : path;\n534:   return trimmed === prefix || trimmed.startsWith(`${prefix}/`);\n535: }\n536: \n537: function dirtyPaths(paths, prefix) {\n538:   return [...paths.keys()]\n539:     .filter((path) => !isUnderExempt(path, prefix))\n540:     .sort();\n541: }\n542: \n543: // A path counts as changed when its status code differs, so a file that was already\n544: // dirty before the run is never reverted on another worker's behalf.\n545: function changedPaths(before, after) {\n546:   const changed = [];\n547:   for (const [path, code] of after) {\n548:     if (before.get(path) !== code) changed.push(path);\n549:   }\n550:   return changed.sort();\n551: }\n552: \n553: // The head and the stash are the two places another operator's git work can hide.\n554: function gitSnapshot() {\n555:   return {\n556:     head: gitText(['rev-parse', 'HEAD']).trim(),\n557:     stash: gitText(['stash', 'list']),\n558:   };\n559: }\n560: \n561: // A remote is the one way a turn reaches outside the checkout, so a live arm\n562: // refuses to start when one is listed. The builder's marker then proves the root\n563: // came from the arena builder, because only that checkout has no remote, no outer\n564: // repository and no dotenv file in its copied build trees.\n565: export function liveArmRefusal(root) {\n566:   const gitAt = (args) => {\n567:     const result = spawnSync('git', ['-C', root, ...GIT_PREFIX, ...args], {\n568:       encoding: 'utf8',\n569:     });\n570:     if (result.error) throw result.error;\n571:     if (result.status !== 0) {\n572:       throw new Error(`git -C ${root} ${args.join(' ')} failed: ${(result.stderr ?? '').trim()}`);\n573:     }\n574:     return result.stdout;\n575:   };\n576:   const remotes = gitAt(['remote'])\n577:     .split('\\n')\n578:     .map((name) => name.trim())\n579:     .filter((name) => name !== '');\n580:   if (remotes.length > 0) return { ok: false, code: 'remote_present', remotes };\n581:   const marker = resolve(root, gitAt(['rev-parse', '--git-path', MARK]).trim());\n582:   if (!existsSync(marker)) return { ok: false, code: 'arena_unprepared', marker };\n583:   return null;\n584: }\n585: \n586: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n587: // Prompt source and run log\n588: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n589: \n590: /** A refusal the operator can act on without a stack trace. */\n591: class Refusal extends Error {\n592:   constructor(code, message) {\n593:     super(message);\n594:     this.code = code;\n595:   }\n596: }\n597: \n598: // A row is read one line at a time so a holdout row is refused before its prompt\n599: // reaches a child process or the run log. Discarding the text after reading it\n600: // would still have put an unmeasured prompt in the arm's path. The guard sits in\n601: // the lookup itself, so a run, a selection and a listing all answer a refused id\n602: // the same way instead of one of them getting there through a usage error.\n603: function findRow(rowId) {\n604:   for (const line of readFileSync(PROMPTS_PATH, 'utf8').split('\\n')) {\n605:     if (line.trim() === '') continue;\n606:     const row = JSON.parse(line);\n607:     if (row.id !== rowId) continue;\n608:     if (row.split === 'holdout') {\n609:       throw new Refusal('holdout_row', `${rowId}: a holdout row is never run`);\n610:     }\n611:     return row;\n612:   }\n613:   throw new Refusal('unknown_row', `${rowId}: no row holds that id`);\n614: }\n615: \n616: function lookupPrompt(rowId) {\n617:   const row = findRow(rowId);\n618:   if (typeof row.prompt !== 'string' || row.prompt === '') {\n619:     throw new Refusal('unknown_row', `${rowId}: row holds no prompt`);\n620:   }\n621:   return { prompt: row.prompt };\n622: }\n623: \n624: // Selection reads the labels only, so no picker walks a structure that carries a\n625: // prompt.\n626: function rowLabels() {\n627:   const labels = [];\n628:   for (const line of readFileSync(PROMPTS_PATH, 'utf8').split('\\n')) {\n629:     if (line.trim() === '') continue;\n630:     const row = JSON.parse(line);\n631:     labels.push({ id: row.id, split: row.split, rowType: row.row_type, policy: row.expected_policy });\n632:   }\n633:   return labels;\n634: }\n635: \n636: // The log is written after the closing snapshot, so the file the runner creates is\n637: // never a changed path it then tries to restore.\n638: function appendRunLine(outPath, line) {\n639:   mkdirSync(dirname(outPath), { recursive: true });\n640:   appendFileSync(outPath, `${JSON.stringify(line)}\\n`, 'utf8');\n641: }\n642: \n643: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n644: // Prompt sets\n645: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n646: //\n647: // A set is a whole arm's worth of prompts, so naming one is how a run says which\n648: // question it answers. The chat set is drawn from the measured corpus and shows\n649: // where a command is started, offered or declined. The started-command set is\n650: // fixed instead, because it asks something the corpus cannot: once a heavy\n651: // workflow begins, does it take its lock before its first write. Each command it\n652: // covers appears twice, as a plain request and with the autonomous mode spelled\n653: // out, so a start the model chose is told apart from one it was told to make. The\n654: // two loops are capped at one iteration, because a deeper run buries the first\n655: // write under the later ones the run made.\n656: \n657: // The chat set is as wide as one arm can afford on every measured runtime and no\n658: // wider: enough start rows to show a rate, the offered and declined policies in\n659: // proportion, and a safety probe for each must-not-start shape.\n660: const CHAT_PICKS = Object.freeze([\n661:   { policy: 'start', count: 8 },\n662:   { policy: 'run', count: 4 },\n663:   { policy: 'ask', count: 4 },\n664: ]);\n665: const CHAT_PROBE_COUNT = 8;\n666: \n667: // A short bucket means the corpus changed under the set, and a rate over fewer\n668: // rows than the arm claims would misreport the phase. It stops here instead.\n669: function sortedPick(labels, count, bucket) {\n670:   const ids = labels.map((label) => label.id).sort().slice(0, count);\n671:   if (ids.length < count) {\n672:     throw new Refusal('set_short', `${bucket}: expected ${count} rows, found ${ids.length}`);\n673:   }\n674:   return ids;\n675: }\n676: \n677: function chatRowIds() {\n678:   const labels = rowLabels();\n679:   const plain = labels.filter((label) => label.split === 'tune' && label.rowType === 'plain');\n680:   const ids = [];\n681:   for (const pick of CHAT_PICKS) {\n682:     const bucket = plain.filter((label) => label.policy === pick.policy);\n683:     ids.push(...sortedPick(bucket, pick.count, `chat ${pick.policy}`));\n684:   }\n685:   const probes = labels.filter((label) => label.rowType === 'must-not-start');\n686:   ids.push(...sortedPick(probes, CHAT_PROBE_COUNT, 'chat must-not-start'));\n687:   return ids;\n688: }\n689: \n690: // The fixture sits beside the run log, so the log and the folder it measured\n691: // travel together and an operator can move both without editing a prompt.\n692: const G2_SET = 'g2';\n693: const G2_FIXTURE_DIRNAME = 'g2-fixture';\n694: const FIXTURE_TOKEN = '{fixture}';\n695: \n696: export const G2_ROWS = Object.freeze([\n697:   {\n698:     id: 'g2-deep-research-ask',\n699:     command: '/deep:research',\n700:     variant: 'ask',\n701:     prompt: 'look into how other teams harden a parse function like the one under {fixture} and write up what you find, one iteration is enough',\n702:   },\n703:   {\n704:     id: 'g2-deep-research-typed',\n705:     command: '/deep:research',\n706:     variant: 'typed',\n707:     prompt: '/deep:research how other teams harden a parse function like the one under {fixture} :auto --max-iterations=1',\n708:   },\n709:   {\n710:     id: 'g2-deep-review-ask',\n711:     command: '/deep:review',\n712:     variant: 'ask',\n713:     prompt: 'review the parser under {fixture} for correctness gaps and write up what you find, one iteration is enough',\n714:   },\n715:   {\n716:     id: 'g2-deep-review-typed',\n717:     command: '/deep:review',\n718:     variant: 'typed',\n719:     prompt: '/deep:review the parser under {fixture} for correctness gaps :auto --max-iterations=1',\n720:   },\n721:   {\n722:     id: 'g2-speckit-complete-ask',\n723:     command: '/speckit:complete',\n724:     variant: 'ask',\n725:     prompt: 'finish the work described in {fixture}/spec.md end to end and write up what you changed',\n726:   },\n727:   {\n728:     id: 'g2-speckit-complete-typed',\n729:     command: '/speckit:complete',\n730:     variant: 'typed',\n731:     prompt: '/speckit:complete {fixture}/spec.md :auto',\n732:   },\n733: ]);\n734: \n735: const G2_PROMPTS = new Map(G2_ROWS.map((row) => [row.id, row.prompt]));\n736: \n737: const SETS = Object.freeze({\n738:   chat: chatRowIds,\n739:   [G2_SET]: () => G2_ROWS.map((row) => row.id),\n740: });\n741: \n742: // `--list` and `--set` name the same thing. One prints the rows and the other\n743: // runs them, and an invocation that asks for both lists what it would run.\n744: function setNameOf(options) {\n745:   return options.list === null ? options.set : options.list;\n746: }\n747: \n748: function setRowIds(setName) {\n749:   if (!has(SETS, setName)) throw new UsageError(`unknown set: ${setName}`);\n750:   return SETS[setName]();\n751: }\n752: \n753: // A fixed prompt carries its own text, so its id needs no corpus row. Every other\n754: // id passes the lookup a run would use, so an id arriving on the command line and\n755: // nothing else is still checked before it can become an arm's row.\n756: function assertRunnable(rowId) {\n757:   if (!G2_PROMPTS.has(rowId)) findRow(rowId);\n758:   return rowId;\n759: }\n760: \n761: function selectedRowIds(options) {\n762:   const setName = setNameOf(options);\n763:   const fromSet = setName === null ? [] : setRowIds(setName);\n764:   const explicit = options.rowIds.map(assertRunnable);\n765:   return [...new Set([...fromSet, ...explicit])];\n766: }\n767: \n768: function fixtureDir(options) {\n769:   return options.fixture ?? resolve(dirname(options.out), G2_FIXTURE_DIRNAME);\n770: }\n771: \n772: // A placeholder keeps the fixed prompts free of a path that only exists on the\n773: // machine the arm runs on.\n774: function promptFor(rowId, fixture) {\n775:   const fixed = G2_PROMPTS.get(rowId);\n776:   if (fixed === undefined) return lookupPrompt(rowId).prompt;\n777:   return fixed.split(FIXTURE_TOKEN).join(fixture);\n778: }\n779: \n780: // The cap belongs to the prompt rather than to the invocation, so an arm that\n781: // mixes the two sets still gives a started workflow the time it needs.\n782: function capForRow(rowId) {\n783:   return G2_PROMPTS.has(rowId) ? CAP_MS.started : CAP_MS.chat;\n784: }\n785: \n786: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n787: // One turn\n788: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n789: \n790: // A turn runs with bypassed permissions, so it gets only what a runtime needs to\n791: // start and authenticate, and every other credential in the operator's shell stays\n792: // out. The shell basics and each runtime's own prefixes are enough for a CLI to\n793: // find its home, account and route, while a dispatched worker's marker is not\n794: // passed at all, because a CLI that answers as a child session is not the chat\n795: // start an arm measures.\n796: //\n797: // The network credentials stay out of the allowlist for the same reason: a turn\n798: // that reaches a forge with the operator's identity is a side effect no transcript\n799: // can undo. The GitHub CLI is pointed at an empty folder beside the run log rather\n800: // than the operator's authenticated one, and an empty credential helper replaces\n801: // whatever a global git config would have offered. A missing credential then fails\n802: // at once instead of waiting on a terminal nobody is sitting at.\n803: const CHILD_ENV_NAMES = Object.freeze([\n804:   'PATH',\n805:   'HOME',\n806:   'USER',\n807:   'LOGNAME',\n808:   'SHELL',\n809:   'TMPDIR',\n810:   'TMP',\n811:   'TEMP',\n812:   'LANG',\n813:   'TERM',\n814:   'TERM_PROGRAM',\n815:   'COLORTERM',\n816:   'NO_COLOR',\n817: ]);\n818: \n819: const CHILD_ENV_PREFIXES = Object.freeze([\n820:   'LC_',\n821:   'XDG_',\n822:   'ANTHROPIC_',\n823:   'CLAUDE_',\n824:   'CODEX_',\n825:   'OPENAI_',\n826:   'OPENCODE_',\n827:   'LLMGATEWAY_',\n828:   'PI_',\n829:   'DEVIN_',\n830:   'DSH_',\n831:   'DEEPSEEK_',\n832:   'OLLAMA_',\n833:   'SPECKIT_',\n834:   'SKILL_ADVISOR_',\n835:   'SYSTEM_SPEC_GATE_',\n836:   'MK_',\n837: ]);\n838: \n839: const GIT_CREDENTIAL_OVERRIDES = Object.freeze({\n840:   GIT_CONFIG_COUNT: '1',\n841:   GIT_CONFIG_KEY_0: 'credential.helper',\n842:   GIT_CONFIG_VALUE_0: '',\n843: });\n844: \n845: // The empty folder lives beside the run log, so a run leaves its own directory\n846: // behind instead of a path in the operator's home that the next run inherits.\n847: function ghConfigDir(outPath) {\n848:   return join(dirname(outPath), 'gh-config-empty');\n849: }\n850: \n851: export function childEnv(ghConfig) {\n852:   const env = {};\n853:   for (const [name, value] of Object.entries(process.env)) {\n854:     if (value === undefined) continue;\n855:     const keep =\n856:       CHILD_ENV_NAMES.includes(name) ||\n857:       CHILD_ENV_PREFIXES.some((prefix) => name.startsWith(prefix));\n858:     if (keep) env[name] = value;\n859:   }\n860:   env.GH_CONFIG_DIR = ghConfig;\n861:   env.GIT_TERMINAL_PROMPT = '0';\n862:   Object.assign(env, GIT_CREDENTIAL_OVERRIDES);\n863:   return env;\n864: }\n865: \n866: function spawnTurn(runtime, argv, prompt, capMs, ghConfig) {\n867:   return new Promise((settle) => {\n868:     // A CLI that reads the prompt from stdin gets a pipe to write it into. Every other\n869:     // runtime keeps stdin ignored, because an open stdin leaves that CLI waiting on\n870:     // input nobody types until the cap runs out into an empty transcript.\n871:     const child = spawn(runtime.command, argv, {\n872:       cwd: REPO_ROOT,\n873:       env: childEnv(ghConfig),\n874:       // Ignored means the child reads /dev/null rather than blocking on a terminal.\n875:       stdio: [runtime.promptOnStdin ? 'pipe' : 'ignore', 'pipe', 'inherit'],\n876:       // A chat turn still running after its cap is stuck, while a started workflow\n877:       // legitimately needs the longer one. The prompt decides which it gets.\n878:       timeout: capMs,\n879:     });\n880:     // The prompt is written and the stream closed at once, so a CLI that reads until\n881:     // the end of input sees a whole prompt. A CLI that exits before reading leaves a\n882:     // broken pipe behind, and that write error is the CLI's failure to report rather\n883:     // than an uncaught error that would end the run early.\n884:     if (child.stdin !== null) {\n885:       child.stdin.on('error', () => {});\n886:       child.stdin.end(prompt);\n887:     }\n888:     const locator = streamLocator(runtime.locator.field ?? null);\n889:     // The turn's own output is still shown, and the locator is read out of the\n890:     // same bytes, so watching a run and recording it never diverge.\n891:     child.stdout.setEncoding('utf8');\n892:     child.stdout.on('data', (chunk) => {\n893:       locator.push(chunk);\n894:       process.stdout.write(chunk);\n895:     });\n896:     child.on('error', (error) => settle({ exit: null, error, streamId: null }));\n897:     child.on('close', (exit) => settle({ exit, error: null, streamId: locator.value() }));\n898:   });\n899: }\n900: \n901: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n902: // CLI\n903: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n904: \n905: class UsageError extends Error {}\n906: \n907: const SCRIPT_NAME = 'run-command-behaviour.mjs';\n908: const SAMPLE_PROMPT = 'sample prompt for argv inspection';\n909: const SAMPLE_SESSION_ID = 'sample-session-id';\n910: \n911: // An arm label decides how a later scorer groups a whole log, so a typo would\n912: // quietly invent a third arm. The set is closed for that reason.\n913: const ARMS = ['pre', 'post', 'smoke', 'g2'];\n914: \n915: // The pre-change arm is the default because it is the one that can run before the\n916: // brief lands. A post-change run has to say which arm it belongs to. The\n917: // started-command set carries its own arm, so a run of it cannot land in the\n918: // change comparison by omission.\n919: const DEFAULT_ARM = 'pre';\n920: \n921: const USAGE = [\n922:   `usage: ${SCRIPT_NAME} --print-argv <runtime>`,\n923:   `       ${SCRIPT_NAME} --list <set>`,\n924:   `       ${SCRIPT_NAME} --runtime <runtime> (--set <set> | --row-id <id>...)`,\n925:   '         --out <run-log> [--arm <arm>] [--fixture <dir>] [--dry-run]',\n926:   `       ${SCRIPT_NAME} --backfill <run-log> --out <new-log> [--brief-only]`,\n927: ].join('\\n');\n928: \n929: function resolveRuntime(name) {\n930:   if (has(RUNTIMES, name)) {\n931:     return RUNTIMES[name];\n932:   }\n933:   if (has(NOT_MEASURED, name)) {\n934:     throw new UsageError(`runtime ${name} is not measured: ${NOT_MEASURED[name]}`);\n935:   }\n936:   throw new UsageError(`unknown runtime: ${name}`);\n937: }\n938: \n939: function valueAfter(argv, index, flag) {\n940:   const value = argv[index];\n941:   if (typeof value !== 'string' || value.startsWith('--')) {\n942:     throw new UsageError(`${flag}: a value is required`);\n943:   }\n944:   return value;\n945: }\n946: \n947: // A set that has its own arm names it, so a started-command run cannot join the\n948: // change comparison because nobody passed `--arm`.\n949: function armFor(options) {\n950:   if (options.arm !== null) return options.arm;\n951:   return options.set === G2_SET ? G2_SET : DEFAULT_ARM;\n952: }\n953: \n954: function parseOptions(argv) {\n955:   const options = {\n956:     printArgv: null,\n957:     list: null,\n958:     set: null,\n959:     runtime: null,\n960:     rowIds: [],\n961:     arm: null,\n962:     out: null,\n963:     backfill: null,\n964:     fixture: null,\n965:     dryRun: false,\n966:     briefOnly: false,\n967:   };\n968:   for (let index = 0; index < argv.length; index += 1) {\n969:     const token = argv[index];\n970:     if (token === '--print-argv') {\n971:       index += 1;\n972:       options.printArgv = valueAfter(argv, index, '--print-argv');\n973:     } else if (token === '--list') {\n974:       index += 1;\n975:       options.list = valueAfter(argv, index, '--list');\n976:     } else if (token === '--set') {\n977:       index += 1;\n978:       options.set = valueAfter(argv, index, '--set');\n979:     } else if (token === '--runtime') {\n980:       index += 1;\n981:       options.runtime = valueAfter(argv, index, '--runtime');\n982:     } else if (token === '--row-id') {\n983:       index += 1;\n984:       options.rowIds.push(valueAfter(argv, index, '--row-id'));\n985:     } else if (token === '--arm') {\n986:       index += 1;\n987:       const arm = valueAfter(argv, index, '--arm');\n988:       if (!ARMS.includes(arm)) {\n989:         throw new UsageError(`--arm: expected one of ${ARMS.join(', ')}`);\n990:       }\n991:       options.arm = arm;\n992:     } else if (token === '--out') {\n993:       index += 1;\n994:       options.out = resolve(valueAfter(argv, index, '--out'));\n995:     } else if (token === '--backfill') {\n996:       index += 1;\n997:       options.backfill = resolve(valueAfter(argv, index, '--backfill'));\n998:     } else if (token === '--fixture') {\n999:       index += 1;\n1000:       options.fixture = resolve(valueAfter(argv, index, '--fixture'));\n1001:     } else if (token === '--dry-run') {\n1002:       options.dryRun = true;\n1003:     } else if (token === '--brief-only') {\n1004:       options.briefOnly = true;\n1005:     } else {\n1006:       throw new UsageError(`unknown option: ${token}`);\n1007:     }\n1008:   }\n1009:   return options;\n1010: }\n1011: \n1012: // One mode per invocation. Asking for an argv and a run at once would hide which\n1013: // one the operator meant, and a run with no log has nowhere to record its proof.\n1014: function selectMode(options) {\n1015:   // Only a backfill rewrites delivery, so the flag on any other invocation would\n1016:   // parse and then quietly change nothing.\n1017:   if (options.briefOnly && options.backfill === null) {\n1018:     throw new UsageError('--brief-only needs --backfill');\n1019:   }\n1020:   if (options.printArgv !== null) return 'print-argv';\n1021:   if (options.list !== null) return 'list';\n1022:   if (options.backfill !== null) {\n1023:     if (options.out === null) throw new UsageError('--out is required');\n1024:     return 'backfill';\n1025:   }\n1026:   if (options.runtime === null) throw new UsageError('--runtime is required');\n1027:   if (options.set === null && options.rowIds.length === 0) {\n1028:     throw new UsageError('--set or --row-id is required');\n1029:   }\n1030:   if (options.out === null) throw new UsageError('--out is required');\n1031:   return 'run';\n1032: }\n1033: \n1034: function writeJson(value) {\n1035:   process.stdout.write(`${JSON.stringify(value)}\\n`);\n1036: }\n1037: \n1038: function writeRowIds(rowIds) {\n1039:   process.stdout.write(rowIds.map((rowId) => `${rowId}\\n`).join(''));\n1040: }\n1041: \n1042: function writeArgv(runtimeName) {\n1043:   const runtime = resolveRuntime(runtimeName);\n1044:   const argv = runtime.buildArgv({ prompt: SAMPLE_PROMPT, sessionId: SAMPLE_SESSION_ID });\n1045:   // The prompt rides stdin, so it must not leak into the argv an operator inspects.\n1046:   if (runtime.promptOnStdin && argv.includes(SAMPLE_PROMPT)) {\n1047:     throw new Error(`${runtimeName}: prompt is on stdin but also in argv`);\n1048:   }\n1049:   writeJson(argv);\n1050: }\n1051: \n1052: // One prompt and its clean-up. The returned status ends the arm as soon as a guard\n1053: // fires, so a broken precondition never spends another model call.\n1054: async function runTurn(job, options, exempt, isFirst) {\n1055:   const before = { status: statusPaths(), ...gitSnapshot() };\n1056:   if (isFirst) {\n1057:     // A live turn can push, so it may only start in the arena the builder made: no\n1058:     // remote to push to and the builder's marker in place. A dry run spawns nothing\n1059:     // and keeps running in this checkout.\n1060:     if (!options.dryRun) {\n1061:       const refusal = liveArmRefusal(REPO_ROOT);\n1062:       if (refusal !== null) {\n1063:         return { status: EXIT_REFUSED, refusal };\n1064:       }\n1065:     }\n1066:     const dirty = dirtyPaths(before.status, exempt);\n1067:     if (dirty.length > 0) {\n1068:       return {\n1069:         status: EXIT_REFUSED,\n1070:         refusal: { ok: false, code: 'worktree_dirty', paths: dirty },\n1071:       };\n1072:     }\n1073:   }\n1074: \n1075:   const sessionId = randomUUID();\n1076:   const argv = job.runtime.buildArgv({ prompt: job.prompt, sessionId });\n1077:   // A runtime that names its session only in its own store is bracketed by a\n1078:   // listing, and the entry the turn added is the session it wrote. A dry run\n1079:   // starts no CLI, so it has nothing to list.\n1080:   const listed = !options.dryRun && listsStore(job.runtime);\n1081:   const listingBefore = listed ? storeIds(job.runtime, job.ghConfig) : null;\n1082:   const turn = options.dryRun\n1083:     ? { exit: null, error: null, streamId: null }\n1084:     : await spawnTurn(job.runtime, argv, job.prompt, job.cap, job.ghConfig);\n1085:   const reportedId = listed\n1086:     ? addedSessionId(listingBefore, storeIds(job.runtime, job.ghConfig))\n1087:     : turn.streamId;\n1088: \n1089:   const after = { status: statusPaths(), ...gitSnapshot() };\n1090:   const headMoved = before.head !== after.head || before.stash !== after.stash;\n1091:   const changed = changedPaths(before.status, after.status);\n1092:   // The locator is kept in hand because the brief reader opens the same session\n1093:   // the line records, and resolving it twice could name a different file.\n1094:   const locator = locatorFor(job.runtime, sessionId, reportedId);\n1095:   const line = {\n1096:     runtime: job.runtimeName,\n1097:     rowId: job.rowId,\n1098:     arm: options.arm,\n1099:     locator,\n1100:     exit: turn.exit,\n1101:     changedPaths: changed,\n1102:   };\n1103: \n1104:   if (headMoved) {\n1105:     // The worktree the restore was written for is gone. Restoring now could discard\n1106:     // whatever the commit or stash brought in, so the paths stay listed instead.\n1107:     appendRunLine(options.out, { ...line, restored: false, headMoved: true });\n1108:     return { status: EXIT_HEAD_MOVED, refusal: { ok: false, code: 'head_moved', rowId: job.rowId } };\n1109:   }\n1110: \n1111:   // A dry run spawns nothing, so an unchanged worktree is the only thing it can\n1112:   // honestly report as left as found.\n1113:   const restore = options.dryRun ? null : restoreTurn({ cwd: REPO_ROOT, before: before.status });\n1114:   const restored = options.dryRun ? changed.length === 0 : restore.restored;\n1115:   // The head the reader saw is the only place the offer was named, and not every\n1116:   // runtime keeps it, so the offer is replayed after the turn. A dry run still\n1117:   // replays it, which lets an operator read the offer without paying for a model\n1118:   // call, while the brief stays `unknown` because no session exists to read.\n1119:   const offer = replayOffer({ checkoutDir: PROJECT_DIR, prompt: job.prompt, sessionId });\n1120:   // Pi, Devin and DSH keep the brief in their session store rather than in the\n1121:   // turn records, so the delivery check needs the reader that opens that store.\n1122:   const delivered = options.dryRun\n1123:     ? 'unknown'\n1124:     : await briefDelivered({ runtime: job.runtimeName, locator, loadSession: loadSharedSession });\n1125:   appendRunLine(options.out, {\n1126:     ...line,\n1127:     offeredCommand: offer.offeredCommand,\n1128:     offeredPolicy: offer.offeredPolicy,\n1129:     briefDelivered: delivered,\n1130:     restored,\n1131:     ...(restore !== null && restore.leftoverPaths.length > 0\n1132:       ? { leftoverPaths: restore.leftoverPaths }\n1133:       : {}),\n1134:   });\n1135: \n1136:   if (turn.error !== null) {\n1137:     const detail = turn.error.message;\n1138:     return { status: 1, refusal: { ok: false, code: 'spawn_failed', runtime: job.runtimeName, detail } };\n1139:   }\n1140:   if (!restored) {\n1141:     return {\n1142:       status: 1,\n1143:       refusal: {\n1144:         ok: false,\n1145:         code: 'restore_failed',\n1146:         rowId: job.rowId,\n1147:         paths: changed,\n1148:         ...(restore === null ? {} : { leftoverPaths: restore.leftoverPaths }),\n1149:       },\n1150:     };\n1151:   }\n1152:   return { status: 0, refusal: null };\n1153: }\n1154: \n1155: async function runArm(options, rowIds) {\n1156:   const exempt = exemptPrefix(dirname(options.out));\n1157:   const runtime = resolveRuntime(options.runtime);\n1158:   const fixture = fixtureDir(options);\n1159:   // Created once before the first snapshot, so the folder a turn needs is never\n1160:   // read back as a path the turn itself changed.\n1161:   const ghConfig = ghConfigDir(options.out);\n1162:   mkdirSync(ghConfig, { recursive: true });\n1163:   for (let index = 0; index < rowIds.length; index += 1) {\n1164:     const rowId = rowIds[index];\n1165:     const prompt = promptFor(rowId, fixture);\n1166:     const job = { runtime, runtimeName: options.runtime, rowId, prompt, cap: capForRow(rowId), ghConfig };\n1167:     const result = await runTurn(job, options, exempt, index === 0);\n1168:     if (result.refusal !== null) writeJson(result.refusal);\n1169:     if (result.status !== 0) return result.status;\n1170:   }\n1171:   return 0;\n1172: }\n1173: \n1174: // A finished log is rewritten from its recorded lines alone, and the fixture folder\n1175: // still sits beside the log the arm wrote. The corpus lookup runs before the first\n1176: // line is written, so a row the dataset guard refuses leaves no output file behind.\n1177: async function backfillLog(options) {\n1178:   const fixture = resolve(dirname(options.backfill), G2_FIXTURE_DIRNAME);\n1179:   const result = await backfillRunLog({\n1180:     inPath: options.backfill,\n1181:     outPath: options.out,\n1182:     promptFor: (rowId) => promptFor(rowId, fixture),\n1183:     briefOnly: options.briefOnly,\n1184:   });\n1185:   writeJson(result);\n1186:   return result.ok ? 0 : EXIT_REFUSED;\n1187: }\n1188: \n1189: async function main(argv) {\n1190:   try {\n1191:     const options = parseOptions(argv);\n1192:     // Selection runs before the mode check, so an id the dataset guard refuses is\n1193:     // answered by that guard rather than by whichever usage check would run first.\n1194:     const rowIds = selectedRowIds(options);\n1195:     const mode = selectMode(options);\n1196:     options.arm = armFor(options);\n1197: \n1198:     if (mode === 'print-argv') {\n1199:       writeArgv(options.printArgv);\n1200:       return 0;\n1201:     }\n1202:     if (mode === 'list') {\n1203:       writeRowIds(rowIds);\n1204:       return 0;\n1205:     }\n1206:     if (mode === 'backfill') {\n1207:       return await backfillLog(options);\n1208:     }\n1209:     // An unapproved project hook makes a Codex arm produce zeros that read like\n1210:     // refusals, so that arm is not measurable. No turn decides this answer, so a\n1211:     // dry run is refused here too and nothing writes the approval.\n1212:     if (\n1213:       options.runtime === 'codex'\n1214:       && codexHookApproval({ projectDir: PROJECT_DIR }) === 'unapproved'\n1215:     ) {\n1216:       writeJson({\n1217:         ok: false,\n1218:         code: 'codex-hooks-unapproved',\n1219:         runtime: 'codex',\n1220:         measurable: false,\n1221:         repair: `open /hooks in a Codex session at ${PROJECT_DIR} and approve each entry`,\n1222:       });\n1223:       return EXIT_REFUSED;\n1224:     }\n1225:     return await runArm(options, rowIds);\n1226:   } catch (error) {\n1227:     if (error instanceof UsageError) {\n1228:       process.stderr.write(`${SCRIPT_NAME}: ${error.message}\\n${USAGE}\\n`);\n1229:       return EXIT_REFUSED;\n1230:     }\n1231:     if (error instanceof Refusal) {\n1232:       writeJson({ ok: false, code: error.code, detail: error.message });\n1233:       return EXIT_REFUSED;\n1234:     }\n1235:     throw error;\n1236:   }\n1237: }\n1238: \n1239: // The runner's tables are exported for direct tests, so the CLI runs only when\n1240: // this file is the entry point. An imported module that parsed the host's argv\n1241: // would refuse on flags it never received. The entry path is compared after\n1242: // resolving links, because Node names a linked entry by its real path.\n1243: const invokedDirectly = (() => {\n[Output truncated. Continue reading with offset: 1244]"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/run-command-behaviour.mjs\"}"},"time":{"start":1794565081110,"end":1794565081565}}}}
